to have this visual grounding.

246
00:11:42,420 --> 00:11:43,710
You have to understand images.

247
00:11:43,710 --> 00:11:49,320
You have to understand graphs
to solve some of these tasks.

248
00:11:49,320 --> 00:11:51,750
So the way that the
environment is represented,

249
00:11:51,750 --> 00:11:54,450
we can think of this environment
as a POMDP environment.

250
00:11:54,450 --> 00:11:57,120
So it is partially observable
Markov decision process.

251
00:11:57,120 --> 00:12:00,800
So you have states, you
have actions, observations,

252
00:12:00,800 --> 00:12:01,800
and transition function.

253
00:12:01,800 --> 00:12:05,210
And so for those of you who've
worked in reinforcement learning

254
00:12:05,210 --> 00:12:08,730
or who have been dealing
with Markov decision, MDPs,

255
00:12:08,730 --> 00:12:10,020
that should be familiar.

256
00:12:10,020 --> 00:12:13,160
And that was the goal to design
the environment in such a way

257
00:12:13,160 --> 00:12:16,790
that it becomes an MDP so
that you can potentially

258
00:12:16,790 --> 00:12:19,070
train your favorite
reinforcement learning

259
00:12:19,070 --> 00:12:20,900
algorithms on this.

260
00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:23,370
So the environment
itself have observations,

261
00:12:23,370 --> 00:12:27,810
whether it's an HTML, whether
it's the visual representation,

262
00:12:27,810 --> 00:12:30,550
like the actual website
itself, and something

263
00:12:30,550 --> 00:12:32,300
that we call the
accessibility tree, which

264
00:12:32,300 --> 00:12:35,600
is a cleaned up
representation of HTML.

265
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:36,950
You have a set of actions.

266
00:12:36,950 --> 00:12:39,350
And the set of actions is
actually a fairly diverse set

267
00:12:39,350 --> 00:12:39,850
of actions.

268
00:12:39,850 --> 00:12:42,010
You can click on a
particular element.

269
00:12:42,010 --> 00:12:43,915
You can hover over a
particular element.

270
00:12:43,915 --> 00:12:45,040
This is an interesting one.

271
00:12:45,040 --> 00:12:47,380
You can type in a
particular element and text.

272
00:12:47,380 --> 00:12:49,990
So you can think, it's not
just a set of discrete actions.

273
00:12:49,990 --> 00:12:52,355
Basically, you can
type in whatever,

274
00:12:52,355 --> 00:12:53,730
if you're searching
for something

275
00:12:53,730 --> 00:12:58,020
or you're feeling certain boxes.

276
00:12:58,020 --> 00:13:02,110
You can have a new tab, so
you can jump to a new website.

277
00:13:02,110 --> 00:13:04,750
You can focus on
a particular tab.

278
00:13:04,750 --> 00:13:06,250
You can close the tab.

279
00:13:06,250 --> 00:13:07,600
You can go a particular URL.

280
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:09,250
You can go forward, go
backwards, scroll up,

281
00:13:09,250 --> 00:13:09,880
scroll down.

282
00:13:09,880 --> 00:13:11,760
And then you have an action
called stop and then answer.

283
00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:14,218
Stop is the action where the
model basically says, OK, I've

284
00:13:14,218 --> 00:13:18,702
solved the task, stop, and then
I give you the final answer.

285
00:13:18,702 --> 00:13:20,160
In this particular
environment, you

286
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:22,030
have deterministic
transition function.

287
00:13:22,030 --> 00:13:25,240
You have the state that
generates the HTML page.

288
00:13:25,240 --> 00:13:27,333
And given the state
and an action,

289
00:13:27,333 --> 00:13:28,500
you generate the next state.

290
00:13:28,500 --> 00:13:31,008
And the next state generation
is actually deterministic.

291
00:13:31,008 --> 00:13:33,300
Basically meaning that if
you want a particular website

292
00:13:33,300 --> 00:13:36,070
and you click on a particular
element or you type something,

293
00:13:36,070 --> 00:13:37,410
there is only one--

294
00:13:37,410 --> 00:13:40,770
there is no stochasticity so
gets you to the next state.

295
00:13:40,770 --> 00:13:42,775
And we also defined
the reward functions.

296
00:13:42,775 --> 00:13:44,330
And the reward
function is primarily

297
00:13:44,330 --> 00:13:48,750
to evaluate when you've
successfully solved a task.

298
00:13:48,750 --> 00:13:50,660
So it's a fairly
sparse reward signal

299
00:13:50,660 --> 00:13:52,520
that we get in this environment.

300
00:13:52,520 --> 00:13:54,920
But let me show
you-- just walk you

301
00:13:54,920 --> 00:13:58,642
through what the model
actually needs to do here.

302
00:13:58,642 --> 00:14:00,410
So what the model
needs to do is it

303
00:14:00,410 --> 00:14:03,510
has to start on the home page.

304
00:14:03,510 --> 00:14:06,555
So here you're trying to
find the cheapest printer.

305
00:14:06,555 --> 00:14:08,180
And you have to
navigate to the printer

306
00:14:08,180 --> 00:14:10,760
category in terms of the
actions that you need to take.

307
00:14:10,760 --> 00:14:12,690
Then you have to sort
by descending order.

308
00:14:12,690 --> 00:14:15,890
So if you click Sort by Price.

309
00:14:15,890 --> 00:14:17,820
Then you click on the
cheapest color printer.

310
00:14:17,820 --> 00:14:19,740
So you have to choose
what's the cheapest one.

311
00:14:19,740 --> 00:14:22,520
So you have to understand how
much each printer cost and you

312
00:14:22,520 --> 00:14:24,090
have to choose the cheapest one.

313
00:14:24,090 --> 00:14:26,130
Then you have to add it
to the shopping cart.

314
00:14:26,130 --> 00:14:27,120
That's another step.

315
00:14:27,120 --> 00:14:28,560
You have to proceed to checkout.

316
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:29,280
That's another step.

317
00:14:29,280 --> 00:14:30,655
Then you have to
fill in the form

318
00:14:30,655 --> 00:14:35,950
correctly with the information
given to you in this image,

319
00:14:35,950 --> 00:14:37,670
and then you have
to place the order.

320
00:14:37,670 --> 00:14:40,020
So if you look at the
entire trajectory here,

321
00:14:40,020 --> 00:14:42,350
it's a fairly
complex trajectory.

322
00:14:42,350 --> 00:14:45,350
The model has to reason about
what it's seeing on the web.

323
00:14:45,350 --> 00:14:48,370
It has to understand what
I mean by the cheapest,

324
00:14:48,370 --> 00:14:51,580
so it has to have a
notion of sorting.

325
00:14:51,580 --> 00:14:54,190
Then it has to really understand
good multimodal abilities

326
00:14:54,190 --> 00:14:57,400
to understand what I'm
seeing in the images.

327
00:14:57,400 --> 00:14:58,370
What's the edges?

328
00:14:58,370 --> 00:15:00,400
And they have to go in
and fill in the address

329
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:01,910
with the right information.

330
00:15:01,910 --> 00:15:06,230
So if you look at it, it's not a
very trivial task to accomplish.

331
00:15:06,230 --> 00:15:09,070
And that's just one
of a thousand tasks

332
00:15:09,070 --> 00:15:10,780
that we've designed.

333
00:15:10,780 --> 00:15:15,991
So in terms of the distribution
of tasks across the websites,

334
00:15:15,991 --> 00:15:18,880
we've looked at how
difficult those tasks are.

335
00:15:18,880 --> 00:15:21,170
I think we have
950 tasks in total,

336
00:15:21,170 --> 00:15:22,550
close to a thousand tasks.

337
00:15:22,550 --> 00:15:24,920
And you can see
about 15% are easy.

338
00:15:24,920 --> 00:15:26,920
Basically what that
means is that it's

339
00:15:26,920 --> 00:15:28,880
easy in terms of
reasoning difficulty,

340
00:15:28,880 --> 00:15:31,310
and easy in terms of
visually understanding

341
00:15:31,310 --> 00:15:32,320
what I'm asking you.

342
00:15:32,320 --> 00:15:34,680
Basically means you have
to do only a few clicks

343
00:15:34,680 --> 00:15:36,220
to actually solve the task.

344
00:15:36,220 --> 00:15:40,380
And then you have this corner
here, which is fairly difficult.

345
00:15:40,380 --> 00:15:41,880
Reasoning difficulty,
so I really

346
00:15:41,880 --> 00:15:44,050
have to understand what
I'm seeing in the image,

347
00:15:44,050 --> 00:15:45,713
and visual difficulty--

348
00:15:45,713 --> 00:15:47,130
and reasoning
difficulty basically

349
00:15:47,130 --> 00:15:49,005
means that I have to
click on a lot of things

350
00:15:49,005 --> 00:15:54,490
and explore to successfully
accomplish this particular task.

351
00:15:54,490 --> 00:15:56,565
So in this particular
environment,

352
00:15:56,565 --> 00:15:58,440
you can actually split
and try to understand,

353
00:15:58,440 --> 00:16:00,270
is it my visual
models are missing,

354
00:16:00,270 --> 00:16:04,830
or is it my
reasoning is missing?

355
00:16:04,830 --> 00:16:09,220
Here's how we're evaluating
in this environment.

356
00:16:09,220 --> 00:16:11,230
So we have execution-based
evaluation.

357
00:16:11,230 --> 00:16:14,320
So, for example, a lot of times,
we want to find the exact match.

358
00:16:14,320 --> 00:16:15,912
So if I'm asking
the question, what's

359
00:16:15,912 --> 00:16:17,370
the ISIN number of
the company that

360
00:16:17,370 --> 00:16:19,990
occupies the largest portion
of Warren Buffett portfolio?

361
00:16:19,990 --> 00:16:23,380
Then I go to Wikipedia, I find
Warren Buffett's portfolio,

362
00:16:23,380 --> 00:16:25,380
and then I get the
information, and I

363
00:16:25,380 --> 00:16:27,280
need to get this exact number.

364
00:16:27,280 --> 00:16:29,310
Sometimes if I
said, add something

365
00:16:29,310 --> 00:16:31,600
like that to the man's wear--

366
00:16:31,600 --> 00:16:33,740
whatever this man is
wearing to my wish list,

367
00:16:33,740 --> 00:16:38,150
then I find something and
I basically have visual QA.

368
00:16:38,150 --> 00:16:40,930
Is this a polo shirt, yes or
no, is this a green shirt,

369
00:16:40,930 --> 00:16:42,892
and so forth.

370
00:16:42,892 --> 00:16:45,130
And then you have--
sometimes when you're looking

371
00:16:45,130 --> 00:16:48,640
for something, you say, it
has to include above 200,

372
00:16:48,640 --> 00:16:53,210
or below 30, above 20,000,
below 30,000, and so forth.

373
00:16:53,210 --> 00:16:57,610
So you can have these
verifiers that once I give you

374
00:16:57,610 --> 00:17:01,030
the final states, I can
actually run these verifiers

375
00:17:01,030 --> 00:17:03,710
and tell you, have you
solved the task or not?

376
00:17:03,710 --> 00:17:06,940
And for a lot of these prompts,
there's only one solution.

377
00:17:06,940 --> 00:17:09,349
Because the environment
is not the full internet,

378
00:17:09,349 --> 00:17:11,200
it's fairly constrained,
but, at least, you

379
00:17:11,200 --> 00:17:14,170
can verify whether your model
actually successfully solved

380
00:17:14,170 --> 00:17:15,680
the task or not.

381
00:17:15,680 --> 00:17:19,569
Now, when we think about
LLMs or visual language,

382
00:17:19,569 --> 00:17:23,200
VLM agents, visual
language models,

383
00:17:23,200 --> 00:17:26,710
the way to think about
this is the following.

384
00:17:26,710 --> 00:17:29,670
One thing I can do is
I can look at the HTML

385
00:17:29,670 --> 00:17:35,010
and try to use that as a
representation into my LLM.

386
00:17:35,010 --> 00:17:38,560
It's also cluttered with a lot
of unnecessary information.

387
00:17:38,560 --> 00:17:40,540
It has a long and
confusing context.

388
00:17:40,540 --> 00:17:43,050
There's been a lot of work with
people doing markups, where

389
00:17:43,050 --> 00:17:46,540
they're trying to identify
the relevant parts of the HTML

390
00:17:46,540 --> 00:17:48,630
and trying to use that.

391
00:17:48,630 --> 00:17:50,585
Another representation
is to use something

392
00:17:50,585 --> 00:17:51,960
that's called
Set-of-Marks, which

393
00:17:51,960 --> 00:17:54,510
is simplified representations.

394
00:17:54,510 --> 00:17:57,240
So what it does is it
basically puts bounding boxes

395
00:17:57,240 --> 00:18:00,035
around elements, around
clickable elements.

396
00:18:00,035 --> 00:18:02,400
So whether I can click
on certain elements

397
00:18:02,400 --> 00:18:05,920
or not, and that's something
that you can get from any HTML.

398
00:18:05,920 --> 00:18:07,500
So that Set-of-Marks
representation

399
00:18:07,500 --> 00:18:09,090
is something that you can do.

400
00:18:09,090 --> 00:18:10,702
Both of these
representations have

401
00:18:10,702 --> 00:18:11,910
advantages and disadvantages.

402
00:18:11,910 --> 00:18:14,130
Set-of-Marks is useful
because, actually, visually,

403
00:18:14,130 --> 00:18:16,600
I understand what I'm
seeing in the image

404
00:18:16,600 --> 00:18:19,080
and what I can click on.

405
00:18:19,080 --> 00:18:21,848
But it's also a very cluttered
type of representation.

406
00:18:21,848 --> 00:18:23,640
And there is a third
type of representation

407
00:18:23,640 --> 00:18:25,210
that people are now
beginning to use,

408
00:18:25,210 --> 00:18:26,770
which is coordinate-based
representation,

409
00:18:26,770 --> 00:18:28,520
where I'm just trying to
predict the coordinate of where

410
00:18:28,520 --> 00:18:29,203
I need to click.

411
00:18:29,203 --> 00:18:30,620
So ultimately,
it's probably going

412
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:32,840
to be a combination
of both HTML,

413
00:18:32,840 --> 00:18:35,870
Set-of-Marks some kind of
coordinate-based representation.

414
00:18:35,870 --> 00:18:39,050
And the way you can think about
these set of representation

415
00:18:39,050 --> 00:18:41,430
is that if, let's say, I
have a regional web page,

416
00:18:41,430 --> 00:18:44,130
I can use the Set-of-Marks
representation,

417
00:18:44,130 --> 00:18:48,320
so I can put the bounding
boxes and have IDs for each box

418
00:18:48,320 --> 00:18:51,990
and have some representation of
what each box is actually doing,

419
00:18:51,990 --> 00:18:55,470
and perhaps have some form
of captioning of each image.

420
00:18:55,470 --> 00:18:59,480
So I have this
Set-of-Marks elements

421
00:18:59,480 --> 00:19:02,485
in the text, as well as the
actual visual representation.

422
00:19:02,485 --> 00:19:04,800
And if I say, if I'm
trying to find this post,

423
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:06,510
navigate to the
comment section of it,

424
00:19:06,510 --> 00:19:09,480
then you pass it to the
LLM or the LLM agent,

425
00:19:09,480 --> 00:19:12,770
and then the agent actually
executes a precise action

426
00:19:12,770 --> 00:19:13,950
to do next.

427
00:19:13,950 --> 00:19:16,850
For example, I want to
click on Element ID 31,

428
00:19:16,850 --> 00:19:20,940
and Element ID 31 is one
of the elements here.

429
00:19:20,940 --> 00:19:24,260
And so, again, the way that we
typically have a representation.

430
00:19:24,260 --> 00:19:25,350
I have a goal.

431
00:19:25,350 --> 00:19:26,820
I get an observation.

432
00:19:26,820 --> 00:19:29,100
I had represented
using Set-of-Marks.

433
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:32,310
That goes to the multimodal
large language models, both

434
00:19:32,310 --> 00:19:34,950
the text and the image
as well as the goal,

435
00:19:34,950 --> 00:19:37,050
and the model generates
a chain of thoughts.

436
00:19:37,050 --> 00:19:38,550
So in this case,
it would generate--

437
00:19:38,550 --> 00:19:39,670
let's think step by step.

438
00:19:39,670 --> 00:19:42,000
The objective is to navigate
to the food, the post,

439
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,280
and so forth.

440
00:19:44,280 --> 00:19:45,900
To navigate to this
listing, I need

441
00:19:45,900 --> 00:19:48,670
to click on the comment link
associated with the sushi.

442
00:19:48,670 --> 00:19:50,670
In summary, my next
action will be--

443
00:19:50,670 --> 00:19:52,575
I'll perform action, click 34.

444
00:19:52,575 --> 00:19:54,700
And, again, that's the
action that you're clicking.

445
00:19:54,700 --> 00:20:01,710
So that's one particular action
that the model is taking.

446
00:20:01,710 --> 00:20:02,800
And then it goes--

447
00:20:02,800 --> 00:20:04,680
once you click on that
particular element,

448
00:20:04,680 --> 00:20:09,370
the simulator will go to the
next web page, the next state,

449
00:20:09,370 --> 00:20:12,310
and then you repeat
this process.

450
00:20:12,310 --> 00:20:15,690
And here, again, the action is
you take a particular action

451
00:20:15,690 --> 00:20:19,410
from the list of actions
that I've mentioned before.

452
00:20:19,410 --> 00:20:22,710
Now, if I look at
baseline models

453
00:20:22,710 --> 00:20:26,150
and try to understand which
models are working well

454
00:20:26,150 --> 00:20:28,310
and which models are
not, what's interesting

455
00:20:28,310 --> 00:20:32,420
is that, if I only use text
only-based representation,

456
00:20:32,420 --> 00:20:35,000
then I hit something
like 90% accuracy.

457
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,310
If I use text plus
caption, so if I

458
00:20:37,310 --> 00:20:40,280
caption any visual
information, any images

459
00:20:40,280 --> 00:20:43,500
that I have on the web
pages, you have improvement.

460
00:20:43,500 --> 00:20:46,110
If I actually try to
use multimodal model,

461
00:20:46,110 --> 00:20:47,760
you get further improvement.

462
00:20:47,760 --> 00:20:50,060
If I use Set-of-Marks
representation,

463
00:20:50,060 --> 00:20:53,070
like, I understand clickable
elements and what I can do,

464
00:20:53,070 --> 00:20:55,590
you get a little bit
of improvement as well.

465
00:20:55,590 --> 00:20:58,860
So you go from 9% to
something like 18%.

466
00:20:58,860 --> 00:21:00,650
And then you get human
level performance,

467
00:21:00,650 --> 00:21:03,707
and the human level performance
hits close to, in this case,

468
00:21:03,707 --> 00:21:05,540
for VisualWebArena, it
was hitting something

469
00:21:05,540 --> 00:21:09,240
like 85%, 86% accuracy.

470
00:21:09,240 --> 00:21:11,690
So there is a big
gap between what

471
00:21:11,690 --> 00:21:15,230
people are capable of doing it
and what existing models are

472
00:21:15,230 --> 00:21:17,150
capable of doing.

473
00:21:17,150 --> 00:21:18,088
This was interesting.

474
00:21:18,088 --> 00:21:19,880
The way that we've
collected the data is we

475
00:21:19,880 --> 00:21:24,108
actually asked students at
CMU to solve these tasks.

476
00:21:24,108 --> 00:21:26,150
We give them the prompts,
we give them the tasks,

477
00:21:26,150 --> 00:21:27,490
we give them the
environment, and they just

478
00:21:27,490 --> 00:21:30,073
interact with the environment,
and then they end in the state,

479
00:21:30,073 --> 00:21:32,650
and then we evaluate using
our execution-based evaluation

480
00:21:32,650 --> 00:21:35,380
whether they successfully
solved the task.

481
00:21:35,380 --> 00:21:37,660
What's interesting
is that we found

482
00:21:37,660 --> 00:21:41,450
that humans were not at 100%.

483
00:21:41,450 --> 00:21:44,080
And what's interesting, a lot
of mistakes that people make

484
00:21:44,080 --> 00:21:45,830
are silly mistakes.

485
00:21:45,830 --> 00:21:49,350
So, for example, if I
ask you to find the--

486
00:21:49,350 --> 00:21:53,300
if I ask you to say, well,
find me the cheapest bicycle

487
00:21:53,300 --> 00:21:56,510
that has these specific
constraints or these conditions.

488
00:21:56,510 --> 00:21:57,680
I want it to be black.

489
00:21:57,680 --> 00:21:59,120
I want it to be of this color.

490
00:21:59,120 --> 00:22:01,250
I want it to be
less than $2,000.

491
00:22:01,250 --> 00:22:04,360
Go find me the cheapest bike.

492
00:22:04,360 --> 00:22:06,190
What people tend
to do, people tend

493
00:22:06,190 --> 00:22:09,910
to go through the
simulator, they

494
00:22:09,910 --> 00:22:12,520
look at-- they find one bike,
they find a second bike,

495
00:22:12,520 --> 00:22:14,020
and then they
basically say, OK, I'm

496
00:22:14,020 --> 00:22:16,280
tired of looking
for anything else.

497
00:22:16,280 --> 00:22:17,420
So I have two bicycles.

498
00:22:17,420 --> 00:22:20,110
I take the cheapest
one and say, I'm done.

499
00:22:20,110 --> 00:22:22,742
Whereas, actually,
there are 10 bicycles

500
00:22:22,742 --> 00:22:23,950
that satisfy the constraints.

501
00:22:23,950 --> 00:22:27,220
So people are just not very good
at information-seeking tasks.

502
00:22:27,220 --> 00:22:30,220
Or, at least, we didn't
pay students enough.

503
00:22:30,220 --> 00:22:35,010
We gave them pizza to go and
spend more than a few minutes

504
00:22:35,010 --> 00:22:36,010
to solve these tasks.

505
00:22:36,010 --> 00:22:38,790
So majority of the
information-seeking task

506
00:22:38,790 --> 00:22:41,110
is where people were
not really good at.

507
00:22:41,110 --> 00:22:44,230
And sometimes people
were a little bit--

508
00:22:44,230 --> 00:22:46,230
They would solve the task,
but they would forget

509
00:22:46,230 --> 00:22:48,605
to click on Place the order.

510
00:22:48,605 --> 00:22:50,980
So they would solve the task,
they would add to the cart,

511
00:22:50,980 --> 00:22:52,730
they find the right
thing, but they forget

512
00:22:52,730 --> 00:22:54,240
to click on Place the order.

513
00:22:54,240 --> 00:22:58,630
And if they forget
to place the order,

514
00:22:58,630 --> 00:23:00,810
then our system basically
says that, well, you

515
00:23:00,810 --> 00:23:02,800
haven't solved it
because you actually

516
00:23:02,800 --> 00:23:05,220
haven't bought the item.

517
00:23:05,220 --> 00:23:08,190
And so this is an
interesting part

518
00:23:08,190 --> 00:23:11,072
because I think that
agentic systems should

519
00:23:11,072 --> 00:23:12,280
be able to solve these tasks.

520
00:23:12,280 --> 00:23:14,670
Agentic system should be
much better at information

521
00:23:14,670 --> 00:23:17,520
seeking and finding
the information

522
00:23:17,520 --> 00:23:20,360
just because it requires
less cognitive load,

523
00:23:20,360 --> 00:23:23,320
whereas people are not
very good at doing that.

524
00:23:23,320 --> 00:23:27,160
If I look at trying
to understand where

525
00:23:27,160 --> 00:23:30,100
we are with these
models, you can

526
00:23:30,100 --> 00:23:33,890
see that Llama, the
older versions of Llama,

527
00:23:33,890 --> 00:23:36,410
they all were really bad.

528
00:23:36,410 --> 00:23:39,020
If I have accessibility
tree plus captions,

529
00:23:39,020 --> 00:23:42,140
a lot of these models
would go higher.

530
00:23:42,140 --> 00:23:44,350
And then once I
have multimodals,

531
00:23:44,350 --> 00:23:46,160
you have much better
representation.

532
00:23:46,160 --> 00:23:49,660
And then once I have the
multimodal models, at that time,

533
00:23:49,660 --> 00:23:51,880
looking at Gemini
Pro and GPT-4, I

534
00:23:51,880 --> 00:23:54,470
think GPT-4 now hits
something like 90%

535
00:23:54,470 --> 00:23:58,870
So these models do climb,
they do better, but they still

536
00:23:58,870 --> 00:24:02,270
far below what people can do.

537
00:24:02,270 --> 00:24:04,570
Even the best models
today are sitting

538
00:24:04,570 --> 00:24:11,110
around 20% accuracy in terms
of solving these tasks.

539
00:24:11,110 --> 00:24:13,960
And here's one example
I wanted to show you.

540
00:24:13,960 --> 00:24:17,640
It's an interesting example
where the GPT model was

541
00:24:17,640 --> 00:24:19,180
able to successfully solve it.

542
00:24:19,180 --> 00:24:21,810
I ask you, I don't like
the author of this image

543
00:24:21,810 --> 00:24:23,770
from the posts,
one of the posts.

544
00:24:23,770 --> 00:24:26,410
Can you help me block them?

545
00:24:26,410 --> 00:24:28,090
And the engine
would actually go,

546
00:24:28,090 --> 00:24:31,000
it would click on the right
things, it would find the post,

547
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:34,620
it would go to the settings,
and through executing

548
00:24:34,620 --> 00:24:36,900
multiple clicks,
it would actually

549
00:24:36,900 --> 00:24:39,428
block a specific user,
just as an example.

550
00:24:39,428 --> 00:24:41,220
You give it this
instruction, and the model

551
00:24:41,220 --> 00:24:44,010
would actually go and do that.

552
00:24:44,010 --> 00:24:46,770
And this has this
notion of-- that I'd

553
00:24:46,770 --> 00:24:49,000
like to think about web
agent architectures,

554
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:52,830
and I'll make that reference
later on for physical agents.

555
00:24:52,830 --> 00:24:54,990
But you can think about
these sort of architectures

556
00:24:54,990 --> 00:24:59,850
where you observe some
input, say, a web page, you

557
00:24:59,850 --> 00:25:02,920
do some form of parsing
of the web page,

558
00:25:02,920 --> 00:25:05,220
whether it's visual
and HTML or some kind

559
00:25:05,220 --> 00:25:08,640
of compressed representation of
HTML plus visual understanding.

560
00:25:08,640 --> 00:25:10,810
You have an objective.

561
00:25:10,810 --> 00:25:13,120
And the objective is
what I need to do,

562
00:25:13,120 --> 00:25:15,890
and a lot of times, what a
lot of these models would do

563
00:25:15,890 --> 00:25:17,715
is they would generate
a high-level plan.

564
00:25:17,715 --> 00:25:19,590
They would generate a
plan of how to execute,

565
00:25:19,590 --> 00:25:21,980
how you should proceed
to solve the task.

566
00:25:21,980 --> 00:25:26,160
And then you execute
the low-level actions,

567
00:25:26,160 --> 00:25:28,200
which is clicking on
a specific element.

568
00:25:28,200 --> 00:25:30,180
And that goes in loop.

569
00:25:30,180 --> 00:25:32,220
You can define the
high-level plan.

570
00:25:32,220 --> 00:25:33,890
You can execute the
low-level action up

571
00:25:33,890 --> 00:25:36,500
until you hit a stop
action, which basically

572
00:25:36,500 --> 00:25:38,390
means that the agent is done.

573
00:25:38,390 --> 00:25:41,954
I think I've solved the task.

574
00:25:41,954 --> 00:25:45,703
And you can think of
planning as a system.

575
00:25:45,703 --> 00:25:47,120
There's been some
work on building

576
00:25:47,120 --> 00:25:50,820
these hierarchical systems
where, for example, step 1,

577
00:25:50,820 --> 00:25:54,650
I can say, navigate me
to a particular category.

578
00:25:54,650 --> 00:25:59,067
Step 2, I want to sort based
on the rating of that category.

579
00:25:59,067 --> 00:26:01,400
And that would be two low-level
actions you're selecting

580
00:26:01,400 --> 00:26:02,665
and then you're sorting.

581
00:26:02,665 --> 00:26:06,110
And then maybe you
select an item under $20,

582
00:26:06,110 --> 00:26:08,252
and then maybe you're
buying a selected item.

583
00:26:08,252 --> 00:26:10,460
You need to buy it, and then
three low-level actions,

584
00:26:10,460 --> 00:26:12,502
you add to the cart, you
proceed to the checkout,

585
00:26:12,502 --> 00:26:14,230
and you do shipping.

586
00:26:14,230 --> 00:26:16,570
So there is some sort
of work right now

587
00:26:16,570 --> 00:26:18,940
that's trying to have the
planning model that actually

588
00:26:18,940 --> 00:26:23,320
plans what you need to do
and then you execute those.

589
00:26:23,320 --> 00:26:25,660
So VisualWebArena,
we think of it

590
00:26:25,660 --> 00:26:28,850
as a step towards building
general purpose agents.

591
00:26:28,850 --> 00:26:30,530
It's a simulator.

592
00:26:30,530 --> 00:26:32,932
So you can evaluate
those models.

593
00:26:32,932 --> 00:26:34,390
Tasks that we
designed are not very

594
00:26:34,390 --> 00:26:35,780
consequential at this point.

595
00:26:35,780 --> 00:26:38,200
They don't really represent
significant economic value

596
00:26:38,200 --> 00:26:39,170
at this point.

597
00:26:39,170 --> 00:26:41,830
And we've designed these
tasks to be simpler

598
00:26:41,830 --> 00:26:44,050
because the current
large language model

599
00:26:44,050 --> 00:26:46,880
agents or AI agents do not
even do well on these tasks.

600
00:26:46,880 --> 00:26:49,600
The gap is still
fairly significant.

601
00:26:49,600 --> 00:26:52,180
And the long-term
question is, can we

602
00:26:52,180 --> 00:26:56,080
automate productive,
economically valuable tasks?

603
00:26:56,080 --> 00:26:59,548

604
00:26:59,548 --> 00:27:02,200
One of the interesting
failure modes

605
00:27:02,200 --> 00:27:03,950
that we see these in models--

606
00:27:03,950 --> 00:27:05,630
and, again, we're fixing those.

607
00:27:05,630 --> 00:27:09,500
A lot of that progresses as the
models become better and better.

608
00:27:09,500 --> 00:27:11,200
But one of the things
that we're seeing

609
00:27:11,200 --> 00:27:15,660
is this notion of long horizon
reasoning and planning.

610
00:27:15,660 --> 00:27:19,680
So models sometimes oscillate
between two web pages,

611
00:27:19,680 --> 00:27:21,330
or sometimes they
get stuck in a loop.

612
00:27:21,330 --> 00:27:23,868
So it's, I'm trying to do
something, I go to this web page

613
00:27:23,868 --> 00:27:26,160
and then I go back and then
I go forward and I go back.

614
00:27:26,160 --> 00:27:28,500
So there's this notion
of persistent memory.

615
00:27:28,500 --> 00:27:31,800
A lot of times,
memory is missing,

616
00:27:31,800 --> 00:27:35,820
so good representation of what
I've done so far is missing.

617
00:27:35,820 --> 00:27:38,120
Of course, I can concatenate
every single web page

618
00:27:38,120 --> 00:27:40,380
I visited with
every single HTML,

619
00:27:40,380 --> 00:27:42,120
but that becomes
fairly cumbersome.

620
00:27:42,120 --> 00:27:44,180
You need to have a good
persistent representation

621
00:27:44,180 --> 00:27:45,110
of the memory.

622
00:27:45,110 --> 00:27:47,720
A lot of times,
models would correctly

623
00:27:47,720 --> 00:27:50,130
start on performing tasks,
and then undoing them.

624
00:27:50,130 --> 00:27:51,090
I don't know why.

625
00:27:51,090 --> 00:27:53,630
It just basically means they
don't have very good reasoning

626
00:27:53,630 --> 00:27:54,660
abilities.

627
00:27:54,660 --> 00:27:58,660
And a lot of times, agents tend
to stop exploration execution

628
00:27:58,660 --> 00:27:59,160
too early.

629
00:27:59,160 --> 00:28:01,980
So instead of exploring what
are the possibilities there,

630
00:28:01,980 --> 00:28:04,280
they actually try to do
something, and then you stop

631
00:28:04,280 --> 00:28:05,420
and you basically execute.

632
00:28:05,420 --> 00:28:08,580
The stop action says like,
I can't solve the task.

633
00:28:08,580 --> 00:28:11,920
I'm not seeing how I
can solve the task.

634
00:28:11,920 --> 00:28:14,480
And there's also some
failures in visual processing.

635
00:28:14,480 --> 00:28:16,960
So sometimes you can
click on the wrong items,

636
00:28:16,960 --> 00:28:20,300
identifying specific items
in complex web pages.

637
00:28:20,300 --> 00:28:23,890
There is also-- they lack a
little bit of spatial reasoning.

638
00:28:23,890 --> 00:28:25,450
If you're asking
the question, what

639
00:28:25,450 --> 00:28:27,470
are the prices of
products in the first row?

640
00:28:27,470 --> 00:28:29,622
You have to understand
what the first row is,

641
00:28:29,622 --> 00:28:31,580
what the second row, and
what's the middle row.

642
00:28:31,580 --> 00:28:35,022
So certain spatial
understanding is missing,

643
00:28:35,022 --> 00:28:36,730
but I think those
things will potentially

644
00:28:36,730 --> 00:28:41,770
get better and better as we
can get the data for training

645
00:28:41,770 --> 00:28:43,210
these models.

646
00:28:43,210 --> 00:28:45,880
So what we need to do,
we need to do a lot more

647
00:28:45,880 --> 00:28:47,620
to close the gap.

648
00:28:47,620 --> 00:28:50,653
Definitely, reasoning and
planning over long horizons.

649
00:28:50,653 --> 00:28:52,070
I'll talk a little
bit about this,

650
00:28:52,070 --> 00:28:55,030
but that's one of the
key failure modes.

651
00:28:55,030 --> 00:28:57,650
We also need to allow
agents to search.

652
00:28:57,650 --> 00:29:00,460
You can think of it as
executing and coordinating

653
00:29:00,460 --> 00:29:04,160
multiple instances in parallel,
and asking for clarifications

654
00:29:04,160 --> 00:29:04,910
and confirmations.

655
00:29:04,910 --> 00:29:07,420
So a lot of times,
search is actually--

656
00:29:07,420 --> 00:29:10,420
you can think of it as test
time inference technique.

657
00:29:10,420 --> 00:29:13,950
Basically tries to explore
possibilities to figure out

658
00:29:13,950 --> 00:29:15,810
what's the right way
to solve the task.

659
00:29:15,810 --> 00:29:17,910
And asking for
clarification, this

660
00:29:17,910 --> 00:29:19,690
is where uncertainty
estimation comes in.

661
00:29:19,690 --> 00:29:22,438
It's also fairly challenging
where, basically, the model

662
00:29:22,438 --> 00:29:23,980
has to understand
that it's confused,

663
00:29:23,980 --> 00:29:25,680
and then it has to
go and ask humans

664
00:29:25,680 --> 00:29:28,150
for clarifications or guidance.

665
00:29:28,150 --> 00:29:31,680
And, obviously, you need strong
visual language code models.

666
00:29:31,680 --> 00:29:34,050
And also, identifying
appropriate levels

667
00:29:34,050 --> 00:29:35,760
of abstractions
for agents like, do

668
00:29:35,760 --> 00:29:38,560
you rely on screenshots,
what you are seeing visually?

669
00:29:38,560 --> 00:29:42,040
Do you rely on HTML, or
maybe you rely on APIs?

670
00:29:42,040 --> 00:29:44,655
There's a lot of websites
that would have APIs.

671
00:29:44,655 --> 00:29:46,780
So maybe instead of actually
going through the web,

672
00:29:46,780 --> 00:29:50,070
you actually go
through API calls.

673
00:29:50,070 --> 00:29:53,580
And, again, I think that
multimodal models here

674
00:29:53,580 --> 00:29:55,980
becomes very important because
a lot of real-world tasks

675
00:29:55,980 --> 00:29:57,820
will require some form
of visual grounding.

676
00:29:57,820 --> 00:30:02,200
So, for example, if you're
interacting with PowerPoint,

677
00:30:02,200 --> 00:30:04,873
if you're interacting with
Photoshop, a lot of it-- yes,

678
00:30:04,873 --> 00:30:06,790
you can do it through
API, but a lot of things

679
00:30:06,790 --> 00:30:08,560
are also done through
visual grounding

680
00:30:08,560 --> 00:30:10,690
and visual understanding.

681
00:30:10,690 --> 00:30:13,480
So we definitely need to
train and build stronger

682
00:30:13,480 --> 00:30:16,420
visual language models.

683
00:30:16,420 --> 00:30:20,523
So that gives me this
notion of VisualWebArena,

684
00:30:20,523 --> 00:30:22,940
which is the environment that
we use for doing evaluation.

685
00:30:22,940 --> 00:30:28,330
Now, let's talk
about that research.

686
00:30:28,330 --> 00:30:32,530
Or we can think of it as,
again, test time inference.

687
00:30:32,530 --> 00:30:38,050
One interesting thing about
agentic agents in general

688
00:30:38,050 --> 00:30:43,010
is that you have this notion of
exponential error compounding.

689
00:30:43,010 --> 00:30:48,170
So, for example, if your
model accurately can--

690
00:30:48,170 --> 00:30:51,560
can be accurate at one
step prediction at 90%,

691
00:30:51,560 --> 00:30:55,330
then if you're
executing 30 actions,

692
00:30:55,330 --> 00:30:59,110
then your accuracy
goes down to 4%.

693
00:30:59,110 --> 00:31:02,450
So you basically need to
be very, very accurate.

694
00:31:02,450 --> 00:31:05,670
So you need to be very precise
in terms of executing each step

695
00:31:05,670 --> 00:31:07,920
in order to be able to
successfully-- solving

696
00:31:07,920 --> 00:31:09,460
long horizon tasks.

697
00:31:09,460 --> 00:31:11,760
And that's pretty
well-known, and any time

698
00:31:11,760 --> 00:31:14,640
you have sequential
decision making process,

699
00:31:14,640 --> 00:31:16,740
you have this issue.

700
00:31:16,740 --> 00:31:20,410
The errors do
compound over time,

701
00:31:20,410 --> 00:31:25,410
which makes it a very,
very difficult task.

702
00:31:25,410 --> 00:31:27,940
So what happens here?

703
00:31:27,940 --> 00:31:32,250
Well, local decisions have
global consequences So,

704
00:31:32,250 --> 00:31:34,330
for example, let's say
I ask you the question,

705
00:31:34,330 --> 00:31:36,480
add coconut milk to my cart.

706
00:31:36,480 --> 00:31:40,560
Well, I can click
on Element ID 31

707
00:31:40,560 --> 00:31:42,100
and that gives me
to the next state,

708
00:31:42,100 --> 00:31:44,190
or maybe I can click
Element ID 24 that

709
00:31:44,190 --> 00:31:47,530
gets me to the next
state, and then so forth.

710
00:31:47,530 --> 00:31:49,598
So it's different choices.

711
00:31:49,598 --> 00:31:50,640
There is a lot of action.

712
00:31:50,640 --> 00:31:52,770
And the action space could
be exponential especially

713
00:31:52,770 --> 00:31:54,062
if I'm doing searching as well.

714
00:31:54,062 --> 00:31:56,760
So I can type text.

715
00:31:56,760 --> 00:32:01,710
And so you can imagine
that each one of these

716
00:32:01,710 --> 00:32:04,070
leads to a trajectory.

717
00:32:04,070 --> 00:32:07,610
Once I execute the
trajectory, then potentially

718
00:32:07,610 --> 00:32:10,740
I can evaluate which one is
right, which one is wrong.

719
00:32:10,740 --> 00:32:14,400
And let's say, basically, this
state gives me the right outcome

720
00:32:14,400 --> 00:32:16,220
and all the other states don't.

721
00:32:16,220 --> 00:32:18,200
And especially if
you're executing

722
00:32:18,200 --> 00:32:22,980
actions, 30 or 40 or 50 actions,
that becomes very challenging.

723
00:32:22,980 --> 00:32:25,520
And if you have a policy
right now that-- you

724
00:32:25,520 --> 00:32:29,330
can think of VLM as your
policy that basically predicts

725
00:32:29,330 --> 00:32:31,070
the probability 0.5,
you should click on

726
00:32:31,070 --> 00:32:32,510
this action, and
probability 0.1,

727
00:32:32,510 --> 00:32:35,120
you should click on this
action, then, obviously,

728
00:32:35,120 --> 00:32:39,458
finding the correct path is
going to be very difficult.

729
00:32:39,458 --> 00:32:43,250
So you basically
have to-- you have

730
00:32:43,250 --> 00:32:48,995
to be able to explore the
environment very efficiently

731
00:32:48,995 --> 00:32:52,130
in this exponential space.

732
00:32:52,130 --> 00:32:56,600
Now, one of the things that
turns out to work okay-ish,

733
00:32:56,600 --> 00:32:59,240
or, at least, is a first
step, is doing search

734
00:32:59,240 --> 00:33:01,500
by repeated sampling.

735
00:33:01,500 --> 00:33:03,260
It's a very simple idea.

736
00:33:03,260 --> 00:33:05,060
Very easy idea.

737
00:33:05,060 --> 00:33:07,570
You sample action
from your model

738
00:33:07,570 --> 00:33:09,865
until you hit the stop action.

739
00:33:09,865 --> 00:33:12,880
So let's say you
sample this path.

740
00:33:12,880 --> 00:33:15,020
And then you evaluate
the resulting trajectory.

741
00:33:15,020 --> 00:33:16,937
So you look at the
trajectory and you can say,

742
00:33:16,937 --> 00:33:19,550
have you solved the task or
did you not solve the task?

743
00:33:19,550 --> 00:33:21,213
If you have verifiers
that can tell you

744
00:33:21,213 --> 00:33:22,630
that you solved
the task, then you

745
00:33:22,630 --> 00:33:25,870
know whether it's
success or not success.

746
00:33:25,870 --> 00:33:30,650
And if it's a not
success, then you repeat.

747
00:33:30,650 --> 00:33:32,650
This would be an
instance of what's

748
00:33:32,650 --> 00:33:34,280
called rejection sampling.

749
00:33:34,280 --> 00:33:38,450
You generate the trajectory,
you look at the outcome,

750
00:33:38,450 --> 00:33:43,195
and if you didn't solve it, you
generate another trajectory.

751
00:33:43,195 --> 00:33:49,400
This is a very simplistic way
called rejection sampling.

752
00:33:49,400 --> 00:33:52,270
You basically sample until
you hit the right answer.

753
00:33:52,270 --> 00:33:56,530
It's fairly inefficient, but
it's surprising that a lot

754
00:33:56,530 --> 00:33:58,780
of work that's happening
right now, for example,

755
00:33:58,780 --> 00:34:03,990
in the space of reasoning,
models like 01, 03 or Deep Seek,

756
00:34:03,990 --> 00:34:06,900
they are essentially
doing exactly this.

757
00:34:06,900 --> 00:34:09,000
They're essentially
doing rejection sampling.

758
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:10,290
I give you the math problem.

759
00:34:10,290 --> 00:34:13,460
I start generating
thinking tokens up

760
00:34:13,460 --> 00:34:15,030
until I get the right answer.

761
00:34:15,030 --> 00:34:17,825
And so I start with
simpler prompts,

762
00:34:17,825 --> 00:34:21,179
I generate sometimes right
answer, sometimes wrong answers.

763
00:34:21,179 --> 00:34:24,860
I use reinforcement learning
to reinforce positive and then

764
00:34:24,860 --> 00:34:28,409
dereinforce negative,
and start scaling it up.

765
00:34:28,409 --> 00:34:30,889
So the simple idea
of rejection sampling

766
00:34:30,889 --> 00:34:33,560
is essentially what's
driving a lot of innovation

767
00:34:33,560 --> 00:34:36,150
right now in reasoning
capabilities.

768
00:34:36,150 --> 00:34:40,159
Which is amazing and was
very surprising to me

769
00:34:40,159 --> 00:34:42,560
that it actually works.

770
00:34:42,560 --> 00:34:45,659
So we've started looking at
exactly the same problem.

771
00:34:45,659 --> 00:34:49,350
But, for us, in this
particular domain,

772
00:34:49,350 --> 00:34:52,199
it's not that you can
generate thinking tokens

773
00:34:52,199 --> 00:34:54,310
and then you basically
get the answer here.

774
00:34:54,310 --> 00:34:56,570
Here you actually have
to execute actions

775
00:34:56,570 --> 00:34:58,360
in the environment
and you change

776
00:34:58,360 --> 00:35:00,320
the state of the environment.

777
00:35:00,320 --> 00:35:03,580
Which makes it much
more difficult.

778
00:35:03,580 --> 00:35:05,480
So here you do
rejection sampling

779
00:35:05,480 --> 00:35:09,020
and hopefully at some point
you'll hit the right answer.

780
00:35:09,020 --> 00:35:14,270
What happens is that repeated
sampling tends to help.

781
00:35:14,270 --> 00:35:16,970
So here is the
number of 10 runs.

782
00:35:16,970 --> 00:35:20,780
And this is what
we call pass at 10.

783
00:35:20,780 --> 00:35:23,170
Which basically
means that if you're

784
00:35:23,170 --> 00:35:28,930
running your model
10 times, in terms

785
00:35:28,930 --> 00:35:32,620
of-- you hit like 15%
probability of getting

786
00:35:32,620 --> 00:35:34,190
the right answer.

787
00:35:34,190 --> 00:35:36,580
So the more you
sample, the probability

788
00:35:36,580 --> 00:35:38,770
that you'll hit the right
answer among these samples

789
00:35:38,770 --> 00:35:43,190
is increasing, and that's
what this graph is showing.

790
00:35:43,190 --> 00:35:49,130
And, however, the space
is exponentially large.

791
00:35:49,130 --> 00:35:51,860
So can we guide
this exploration?

792
00:35:51,860 --> 00:35:54,820
And the key idea here is
to apply the value function

793
00:35:54,820 --> 00:35:56,970
to intermediate nodes.

794
00:35:56,970 --> 00:36:01,680
It's a very old idea, but
you can apply it, actually,

795
00:36:01,680 --> 00:36:03,580
in this space as well.

796
00:36:03,580 --> 00:36:06,150
So how does this work?

797
00:36:06,150 --> 00:36:10,380
So the idea is to, basically,
do a form of a best-first search

798
00:36:10,380 --> 00:36:12,850
algorithm with a few tweaks.

799
00:36:12,850 --> 00:36:15,190
So what are the ingredients
of the algorithm?

800
00:36:15,190 --> 00:36:17,440
So the baseline agent
proposes actions.

801
00:36:17,440 --> 00:36:19,830
The baseline agent
actually says, OK,

802
00:36:19,830 --> 00:36:22,133
here's two actions
that I can take.

803
00:36:22,133 --> 00:36:23,550
These are the most
probable action

804
00:36:23,550 --> 00:36:25,950
according to the
base model, according

805
00:36:25,950 --> 00:36:28,740
to your original policy.

806
00:36:28,740 --> 00:36:33,016
You have to have a way to
backtrack in the environment.

807
00:36:33,016 --> 00:36:35,920
So that basically means
that, in this state,

808
00:36:35,920 --> 00:36:39,330
you want to be able to
backtrack to the original state.

809
00:36:39,330 --> 00:36:41,640
This is sometimes
difficult to do, especially

810
00:36:41,640 --> 00:36:46,120
in web environments
because some actions,

811
00:36:46,120 --> 00:36:48,870
you can't backtrack from,
and these actions that we

812
00:36:48,870 --> 00:36:52,320
call destructive actions.

813
00:36:52,320 --> 00:36:56,640
If I place the order, I can't
just go back and unplace it.

814
00:36:56,640 --> 00:36:58,050
That's just one example.

815
00:36:58,050 --> 00:36:59,880
So we'll talk a
little bit about this.

816
00:36:59,880 --> 00:37:01,890
And then you also have
to have a value function.

817
00:37:01,890 --> 00:37:03,473
You can think of a
value function that

818
00:37:03,473 --> 00:37:07,280
takes the input, whatever
your presentation is, plus

819
00:37:07,280 --> 00:37:11,480
it takes a sequence of previous
observations or previous steps

820
00:37:11,480 --> 00:37:14,810
and outputs a score.

821
00:37:14,810 --> 00:37:18,740
And you can think a value
function essentially

822
00:37:18,740 --> 00:37:23,630
looking at the expected
value, expected reward.

823
00:37:23,630 --> 00:37:27,290
In our settings, think of it as
the probability of successfully

824
00:37:27,290 --> 00:37:28,212
solving the task.

825
00:37:28,212 --> 00:37:30,170
So it's a number between
0 and 1 that basically

826
00:37:30,170 --> 00:37:34,470
says, if I'm in this state
and my value function is 0.5,

827
00:37:34,470 --> 00:37:39,060
then the probability of
me succeeding is 50%.

828
00:37:39,060 --> 00:37:41,090
And if the value
function in this state

829
00:37:41,090 --> 00:37:44,490
is 0.3, then the probability
of me succeeding is 30%.

830
00:37:44,490 --> 00:37:46,640
So maybe this state is
better than this state.

831
00:37:46,640 --> 00:37:49,140
So you have to have a
good value function,

832
00:37:49,140 --> 00:37:52,590
and these are values
that you can associate.

833
00:37:52,590 --> 00:37:55,530
And what we've done in this
work is we're basically

834
00:37:55,530 --> 00:37:57,130
using LLM as a judge.

835
00:37:57,130 --> 00:38:00,700
So in this case,
you take a GPT-4o,

836
00:38:00,700 --> 00:38:03,550
you take the full trace,
the full trajectory,

837
00:38:03,550 --> 00:38:08,490
and you're asking
the model to say

838
00:38:08,490 --> 00:38:11,680
how good the state is in terms
of solving a particular task.

839
00:38:11,680 --> 00:38:16,870
So there's a way to use
this LLM as a judge.

840
00:38:16,870 --> 00:38:18,720
There's a few tricks
of self-consistency

841
00:38:18,720 --> 00:38:19,690
that you can use.

842
00:38:19,690 --> 00:38:23,040
But essentially, you can just
use a large language model

843
00:38:23,040 --> 00:38:24,940
as a judge to judge each state.

844
00:38:24,940 --> 00:38:29,310
Ultimately, I do believe that
the next-generation models will

845
00:38:29,310 --> 00:38:30,880
create their own
value functions.

846
00:38:30,880 --> 00:38:33,060
You'll train your own value
function that basically

847
00:38:33,060 --> 00:38:35,340
will guide this exploration.

848
00:38:35,340 --> 00:38:35,860
OK.

849
00:38:35,860 --> 00:38:37,360
So how does this work?

850
00:38:37,360 --> 00:38:40,210
Let's say that I
give you a task.

851
00:38:40,210 --> 00:38:41,670
The task construction
is, can you

852
00:38:41,670 --> 00:38:44,970
add this and the other canned
fruit of the same brand

853
00:38:44,970 --> 00:38:47,790
that looks like this,
but red instead of brown

854
00:38:47,790 --> 00:38:50,680
to the comparison page?

855
00:38:50,680 --> 00:38:51,930
So how does this work?

856
00:38:51,930 --> 00:38:55,790
Well, you take the action,
and then at this point

857
00:38:55,790 --> 00:38:57,870
you have three possible
actions to take.

858
00:38:57,870 --> 00:38:59,840
They all have a
probability-- they all have

859
00:38:59,840 --> 00:39:02,200
the value function of 0.45.

860
00:39:02,200 --> 00:39:04,770
So you pick one of
them, you proceed,

861
00:39:04,770 --> 00:39:07,340
and then you go to the
next kind of state,

862
00:39:07,340 --> 00:39:08,880
and your value
function decreases.

863
00:39:08,880 --> 00:39:10,797
So you basically say,
OK, I'm actually-- maybe

864
00:39:10,797 --> 00:39:13,130
I'm on the wrong path
to solving this task.

865
00:39:13,130 --> 00:39:16,940
And then I backtrack and
I take another action.

866
00:39:16,940 --> 00:39:18,630
So I backtrack to
the previous state,

867
00:39:18,630 --> 00:39:20,550
and I take the other action.

868
00:39:20,550 --> 00:39:23,550
And let's say here I go in,
my probability increases.

869
00:39:23,550 --> 00:39:25,830
I go in, my
probability increases.

870
00:39:25,830 --> 00:39:28,130
And then I hit a
particular state

871
00:39:28,130 --> 00:39:30,560
where I'm not actually
going to solve

872
00:39:30,560 --> 00:39:33,260
this task because my
value of the state

873
00:39:33,260 --> 00:39:36,140
is actually pretty
small because I ended up

874
00:39:36,140 --> 00:39:37,940
in a very wrong state.

875
00:39:37,940 --> 00:39:41,670
So then I have to be able to
backtrack again from this state.

876
00:39:41,670 --> 00:39:45,320
I have to be able to backtrack
back to the original state,

877
00:39:45,320 --> 00:39:51,550
and then go and, again,
take the next states,

878
00:39:51,550 --> 00:39:55,730
and then if it's bad, backtrack
and eventually solve the task.

879
00:39:55,730 --> 00:40:01,630
So you can see that
it's more expensive,

880
00:40:01,630 --> 00:40:04,190
there are different
ways of exploring,

881
00:40:04,190 --> 00:40:07,480
but the key here that drives
a lot of this exploration

882
00:40:07,480 --> 00:40:09,590
is the value of the state.

883
00:40:09,590 --> 00:40:13,120
Which basically is telling you
how good the particular state is

884
00:40:13,120 --> 00:40:14,720
in terms of solving the task.

885
00:40:14,720 --> 00:40:18,320
And you can spot the errors
early on and say, no, no, no,

886
00:40:18,320 --> 00:40:21,580
backtrack and restart again.

887
00:40:21,580 --> 00:40:25,570
So it's a little bit better
than just doing random rejection

888
00:40:25,570 --> 00:40:31,990
sampling because here you're
doing guided exploration.

889
00:40:31,990 --> 00:40:36,430
It's very similar to-- some of
you are familiar with AlphaGo

890
00:40:36,430 --> 00:40:37,240
setting, right?

891
00:40:37,240 --> 00:40:40,840
Where you basically have
an environment where

892
00:40:40,840 --> 00:40:42,820
you have a value of
the state and then

893
00:40:42,820 --> 00:40:45,050
you try to explore
which actions to take,

894
00:40:45,050 --> 00:40:47,630
and you evaluate those states
given the value function

895
00:40:47,630 --> 00:40:49,880
that you have.

896
00:40:49,880 --> 00:40:52,610
And so if we look at the
results, what we found

897
00:40:52,610 --> 00:40:59,210
is that, for Web Arena, there
is pretty decent improvement

898
00:40:59,210 --> 00:41:01,790
in terms of the ability
to solve these tasks.

899
00:41:01,790 --> 00:41:05,510
For the VisualWebArena, we
also see quite reasonable

900
00:41:05,510 --> 00:41:08,040
improvements by using
this exploration.

901
00:41:08,040 --> 00:41:10,708
It's more expensive.

902
00:41:10,708 --> 00:41:15,530
And so we do see that
these search at test time

903
00:41:15,530 --> 00:41:19,710
actually improves model's
ability to solve the task,

904
00:41:19,710 --> 00:41:21,680
or, at least, to solve
some of the tasks.

905
00:41:21,680 --> 00:41:24,230
You can still see that
we're still very far away

906
00:41:24,230 --> 00:41:27,710
from hitting 90% accuracy.

907
00:41:27,710 --> 00:41:30,290
But nonetheless, it's
actually a progress, where

908
00:41:30,290 --> 00:41:32,940
we're progressively improving.

909
00:41:32,940 --> 00:41:38,450
And we do see that if you
look at the success rate,

910
00:41:38,450 --> 00:41:40,920
c 0 indicates no search.

911
00:41:40,920 --> 00:41:44,040
And then the more node
expansions you have,

912
00:41:44,040 --> 00:41:45,830
the better your performances.

913
00:41:45,830 --> 00:41:48,470
And then you can also
look at the success rate

914
00:41:48,470 --> 00:41:52,170
relative to how
you want to expand.

915
00:41:52,170 --> 00:41:55,640
Do you control the depth
versus the breadth,

916
00:41:55,640 --> 00:41:59,930
and, again, how much you
want to explore versus

917
00:41:59,930 --> 00:42:02,360
what's the branching factor?

918
00:42:02,360 --> 00:42:06,690
And then if we keep exploring
and doing the branching,

919
00:42:06,690 --> 00:42:09,320
then the accuracy tends to go
up just because the model is

920
00:42:09,320 --> 00:42:14,637
able to explore possible
different possibilities

921
00:42:14,637 --> 00:42:15,720
or different environments.

922
00:42:15,720 --> 00:42:19,760
A lot of times what we
see in these settings

923
00:42:19,760 --> 00:42:23,590
is that, if I ask it to
buy a blue polo shirt,

924
00:42:23,590 --> 00:42:25,650
if you don't do the search,
the model, let's say,

925
00:42:25,650 --> 00:42:27,650
goes and let's say it
clicks on the wrong thing,

926
00:42:27,650 --> 00:42:31,850
and it ends up on a web page
that only has blue polo shirts.

927
00:42:31,850 --> 00:42:33,350
So you're asking
black polo shirt

928
00:42:33,350 --> 00:42:35,000
and gives you blue polo shirts.

929
00:42:35,000 --> 00:42:36,500
And then the model
says, well, there

930
00:42:36,500 --> 00:42:38,930
are no black polo
shirts because I'm only

931
00:42:38,930 --> 00:42:40,080
seeing blue polo shirts.

932
00:42:40,080 --> 00:42:41,090
I can't solve the task.

933
00:42:41,090 --> 00:42:42,390
Done.

934
00:42:42,390 --> 00:42:44,430
And, obviously, this
is the case that if you

935
00:42:44,430 --> 00:42:48,190
can backtrack and explore, you
will find the right answer.

936
00:42:48,190 --> 00:42:51,090
So this notion of
search actually

937
00:42:51,090 --> 00:42:55,020
becomes fairly
important and such.

938
00:42:55,020 --> 00:42:57,810
And we also found that
having a good value function

939
00:42:57,810 --> 00:43:00,231
is essential.

940
00:43:00,231 --> 00:43:03,438
There is still a lot of headroom
for improving the base policy.

941
00:43:03,438 --> 00:43:05,230
Obviously, your base
policy has to be good.

942
00:43:05,230 --> 00:43:06,960
It can't just take
random actions.

943
00:43:06,960 --> 00:43:08,448
If your base policy is--

944
00:43:08,448 --> 00:43:10,240
if your base model is
actually pretty weak,

945
00:43:10,240 --> 00:43:12,720
then you'll just be
taking random actions

946
00:43:12,720 --> 00:43:17,940
and there's only that much
you can do with exploration,

947
00:43:17,940 --> 00:43:20,842
but you need to improve both.

948
00:43:20,842 --> 00:43:25,170
And you can see, if you use
different value functions,

949
00:43:25,170 --> 00:43:27,270
then the performance
actually improves.

950
00:43:27,270 --> 00:43:31,150
With no search, if you say
lava, which is a weaker version,

951
00:43:31,150 --> 00:43:32,290
you get the improvement.

952
00:43:32,290 --> 00:43:37,530
If you use something for all
type of models, then you have--

953
00:43:37,530 --> 00:43:39,430
so if you have a
better value function,

954
00:43:39,430 --> 00:43:41,380
kind of looks at
the full trajectory,

955
00:43:41,380 --> 00:43:45,210
then you have improvements
in performance.

956
00:43:45,210 --> 00:43:47,390
And the interesting
thing is that using

957
00:43:47,390 --> 00:43:51,540
LLM as a judge, judging whether
the trajectory is good or bad,

958
00:43:51,540 --> 00:43:54,590
is much easier than predicting
what the next action

959
00:43:54,590 --> 00:43:59,070
you should take or what the
next element you should click on

960
00:43:59,070 --> 00:44:00,570
and what you should
be searching on.

961
00:44:00,570 --> 00:44:06,260
And so that's why starting--
using value function as an LLM,

962
00:44:06,260 --> 00:44:08,640
initially, I think it's
a reasonable thing to do,

963
00:44:08,640 --> 00:44:11,400
but, obviously, you want
to be able to improve this.

964
00:44:11,400 --> 00:44:13,050
Here's just some examples.

965
00:44:13,050 --> 00:44:15,950
You can basically say, I
recall seeing this exact item

966
00:44:15,950 --> 00:44:16,800
on the side.

967
00:44:16,800 --> 00:44:18,960
Help me find the most
recent post of it.

968
00:44:18,960 --> 00:44:21,250
I recall seeing it in either
collectibles or antiques

969
00:44:21,250 --> 00:44:21,750
section.

970
00:44:21,750 --> 00:44:25,020
So the models would go
to the starting state,

971
00:44:25,020 --> 00:44:27,350
it would explore, and
then eventually find

972
00:44:27,350 --> 00:44:30,302
that particular posting.

973
00:44:30,302 --> 00:44:32,670
Or this one where I can
say, hey, I like this.

974
00:44:32,670 --> 00:44:35,930
I need something like
this in my apartment.

975
00:44:35,930 --> 00:44:38,190
Can you add one to my wish list?

976
00:44:38,190 --> 00:44:41,490
So the model have to
go, it has to search

977
00:44:41,490 --> 00:44:44,790
through different environments,
find this specific item,

978
00:44:44,790 --> 00:44:47,790
and add it to your wish list.

979
00:44:47,790 --> 00:44:50,357
It would be great if you
have something like that.

980
00:44:50,357 --> 00:44:52,440
You take a picture of
something, and you basically

981
00:44:52,440 --> 00:44:55,420
tell your agent, hey, go
and find it on Amazon,

982
00:44:55,420 --> 00:44:59,250
Best Buy, or wherever, get
me the best price for this,

983
00:44:59,250 --> 00:45:03,600
and see where I can buy it.

984
00:45:03,600 --> 00:45:05,640
Just as an example.

985
00:45:05,640 --> 00:45:08,620
Now, in terms of
limitations of this,

986
00:45:08,620 --> 00:45:12,210
well, search is pretty slow.

987
00:45:12,210 --> 00:45:17,080
So you, obviously, need a fast
way of doing backtracking.

988
00:45:17,080 --> 00:45:19,930
We did the backtracking
in a relatively naive way.

989
00:45:19,930 --> 00:45:22,440
You store all actions
in a queue and then

990
00:45:22,440 --> 00:45:24,600
take them again to get
to the original state.

991
00:45:24,600 --> 00:45:26,885
So if you've taking
20 actions, you

992
00:45:26,885 --> 00:45:29,010
store what you've done so
far, and then to go back,

993
00:45:29,010 --> 00:45:31,450
you rerun that again.

994
00:45:31,450 --> 00:45:34,300
So it's not very
efficient what we've done.

995
00:45:34,300 --> 00:45:38,570
I think it's just requires
infrastructure work.

996
00:45:38,570 --> 00:45:40,490
And the other interesting
piece is dealing

997
00:45:40,490 --> 00:45:42,330
with destructive actions.

998
00:45:42,330 --> 00:45:46,790
Certain things on the web
are very difficult to undo.

999
00:45:46,790 --> 00:45:48,860
Especially if you're
operating on the real web

1000
00:45:48,860 --> 00:45:50,930
for testing these models.

1001
00:45:50,930 --> 00:45:53,850
You can't order the item
and then unorder it.

1002
00:45:53,850 --> 00:45:56,510
It's not like you can order the
item, and then your model says,

1003
00:45:56,510 --> 00:45:59,430
you know what, that was
actually the wrong order.

1004
00:45:59,430 --> 00:46:01,080
Let's try ordering
something else.

1005
00:46:01,080 --> 00:46:03,470
You can't do that.

1006
00:46:03,470 --> 00:46:06,080
And so the other thing that
you can't, unfortunately,

1007
00:46:06,080 --> 00:46:08,390
do in the real web
is it's very hard

1008
00:46:08,390 --> 00:46:12,900
to do some form of
learning in the real web.

1009
00:46:12,900 --> 00:46:15,740
Because anything that changes
the state of the environment

1010
00:46:15,740 --> 00:46:20,600
is actually fairly difficult.
I can't go on the United

1011
00:46:20,600 --> 00:46:24,350
and start booking flights from
Pittsburgh to San Francisco

1012
00:46:24,350 --> 00:46:26,550
to learn how to
book the flights.

1013
00:46:26,550 --> 00:46:29,580
Because if I'm exploring
and I'm saying,

1014
00:46:29,580 --> 00:46:32,270
why don't I click here, well,
maybe I should click here,

1015
00:46:32,270 --> 00:46:34,870
and then maybe I should
try to book this flight

1016
00:46:34,870 --> 00:46:37,370
and then maybe it's wrong and
try to book this other flight.

1017
00:46:37,370 --> 00:46:39,970
And so it becomes a
little bit tricky to do

1018
00:46:39,970 --> 00:46:42,160
because, essentially,
United will block you.

1019
00:46:42,160 --> 00:46:44,595
Or United will
basically say, oh,

1020
00:46:44,595 --> 00:46:45,970
there's so many
people are trying

1021
00:46:45,970 --> 00:46:48,380
to book flights from
Pittsburgh to San Francisco.

1022
00:46:48,380 --> 00:46:51,310
Maybe we should
increase the prices.

1023
00:46:51,310 --> 00:46:53,560
So you have to be careful
how you're actually

1024
00:46:53,560 --> 00:46:56,710
operating in the real world.

1025
00:46:56,710 --> 00:46:58,510
So what's the current work?

1026
00:46:58,510 --> 00:47:01,040
Obviously, search as a
policy improvement function.

1027
00:47:01,040 --> 00:47:04,840
You can use search as a way
of finding the correct trace

1028
00:47:04,840 --> 00:47:06,320
or the correct trajectory.

1029
00:47:06,320 --> 00:47:08,935
And you can use this potentially
either in reinforcement

1030
00:47:08,935 --> 00:47:11,470
type of learning algorithm
or actually in SFT

1031
00:47:11,470 --> 00:47:15,820
to try to fine-tuning this.

1032
00:47:15,820 --> 00:47:17,865
Improving value
function by fine-tuning

1033
00:47:17,865 --> 00:47:18,740
instead of prompting.

1034
00:47:18,740 --> 00:47:22,900
So if you can improve the value
function by using Monte Carlo

1035
00:47:22,900 --> 00:47:26,680
rollouts, you can do that.

1036
00:47:26,680 --> 00:47:29,740
Obviously, exploring tradeoffs
between improving baseline

1037
00:47:29,740 --> 00:47:31,930
agent, how much your
base model has to be,

1038
00:47:31,930 --> 00:47:35,260
versus how much more search you
have to do at inference time.

1039
00:47:35,260 --> 00:47:38,280
And today we see this
notion of test time

1040
00:47:38,280 --> 00:47:42,850
inference scaling loss where,
well, if I do search for more,

1041
00:47:42,850 --> 00:47:44,410
I can probably get
a better answer.

1042
00:47:44,410 --> 00:47:46,740
And I think that
it's true, but it's--

1043
00:47:46,740 --> 00:47:48,340
both models need to improve.

1044
00:47:48,340 --> 00:47:51,010
Your base model
needs to be better,

1045
00:47:51,010 --> 00:47:54,180
and also search needs
to be more efficient.

1046
00:47:54,180 --> 00:47:57,220
And, obviously, one of
the biggest questions,

1047
00:47:57,220 --> 00:47:59,280
if we don't have a
simulator of the web

1048
00:47:59,280 --> 00:48:02,260
environment, how can we
collect data at scale?

1049
00:48:02,260 --> 00:48:05,640
And that's one of the
interesting problems

1050
00:48:05,640 --> 00:48:08,850
that we also started
looking at, what can we do

1051
00:48:08,850 --> 00:48:11,320
and how we can collect
the data at scale?

1052
00:48:11,320 --> 00:48:15,360
Because the interesting thing
about agentic models in general

1053
00:48:15,360 --> 00:48:18,120
is that it's the kind of
data that's very difficult

1054
00:48:18,120 --> 00:48:19,690
to find on the web.

1055
00:48:19,690 --> 00:48:21,810
So what we see with
existing LLMs is

1056
00:48:21,810 --> 00:48:26,170
that I can crawl the web, I
can scrape images and HTML,

1057
00:48:26,170 --> 00:48:28,350
and then I can pre-train
these models just doing

1058
00:48:28,350 --> 00:48:30,116
the next token prediction.

1059
00:48:30,116 --> 00:48:33,010
For agentic work,
it's very difficult.

1060
00:48:33,010 --> 00:48:35,560
It's very difficult for
me to say, hey, give me

1061
00:48:35,560 --> 00:48:37,450
a whole bunch of
examples and show me

1062
00:48:37,450 --> 00:48:39,820
how I should be booking
my flight from Pittsburgh

1063
00:48:39,820 --> 00:48:42,290
to San Francisco.

1064
00:48:42,290 --> 00:48:45,760
Or show me, in
this setting, like,

1065
00:48:45,760 --> 00:48:47,660
hey, I like something like this.

1066
00:48:47,660 --> 00:48:49,010
Add me to my wish list.

1067
00:48:49,010 --> 00:48:51,700
It's not like you can find
a lot of these examples

1068
00:48:51,700 --> 00:48:57,160
on the web of examples
of people showing you

1069
00:48:57,160 --> 00:48:58,720
how to solve these tasks.

1070
00:48:58,720 --> 00:49:02,770
So this notion of giving the
task and the full trajectory

1071
00:49:02,770 --> 00:49:05,800
of how you should take
actions and solve the task

1072
00:49:05,800 --> 00:49:09,680
is actually very limited
data that we have today.

1073
00:49:09,680 --> 00:49:12,070
In fact, we don't
have any of that data.

1074
00:49:12,070 --> 00:49:14,620
A lot of companies, they're
essentially collecting this data

1075
00:49:14,620 --> 00:49:17,950
in-house by asking people
to solve these tasks

1076
00:49:17,950 --> 00:49:20,720
and do massive annotations.

1077
00:49:20,720 --> 00:49:23,380
So the question for
us was, how can we

1078
00:49:23,380 --> 00:49:24,890
collect the data at scale?

1079
00:49:24,890 --> 00:49:26,600
And that gives me
to the third point,

1080
00:49:26,600 --> 00:49:30,610
which is, how can we,
at least, try thinking

1081
00:49:30,610 --> 00:49:33,930
about what we can do here?

1082
00:49:33,930 --> 00:49:37,080
So as I've mentioned
before, agents

1083
00:49:37,080 --> 00:49:39,760
suffer from a data problem.

1084
00:49:39,760 --> 00:49:44,310
And you can see the gap
is still pretty big.

1085
00:49:44,310 --> 00:49:47,670
And when we think about LLMs,
the way that we're training them

1086
00:49:47,670 --> 00:49:50,530
today, a lot of times
they're often trained online.

1087
00:49:50,530 --> 00:49:54,360
I just collect a bunch of
data, and then I deploy them

1088
00:49:54,360 --> 00:49:56,110
as zero-shots as agents.

1089
00:49:56,110 --> 00:49:58,060
And then I collect
a lot of data,

1090
00:49:58,060 --> 00:50:00,943
I do some form of
supervised fine-tuning,

1091
00:50:00,943 --> 00:50:02,860
at least, that's what's
happening in academia,

1092
00:50:02,860 --> 00:50:07,605
and then I try to deploy
them and see how they work.

1093
00:50:07,605 --> 00:50:12,870
The question is, can we
somehow go on the real web

1094
00:50:12,870 --> 00:50:17,760
and generate synthetic tasks to
unlock this large-scale training

1095
00:50:17,760 --> 00:50:19,357
of agents?

1096
00:50:19,357 --> 00:50:27,900
And the question is, how can
we, again, get these models

1097
00:50:27,900 --> 00:50:31,850
to generate something synthetic
and actually build something

1098
00:50:31,850 --> 00:50:34,250
that we can train on
and improve our edges

1099
00:50:34,250 --> 00:50:38,970
without human intervention, or
without actually task generated

1100
00:50:38,970 --> 00:50:39,470
by humans?

1101
00:50:39,470 --> 00:50:45,290
Because it's fairly expensive to
ask humans to generate or create

1102
00:50:45,290 --> 00:50:47,360
those tasks.

1103
00:50:47,360 --> 00:50:50,030
So one of the things that
we've been trying to build

1104
00:50:50,030 --> 00:50:52,250
is we've been trying to
build this internet scale

1105
00:50:52,250 --> 00:50:57,932
training for agents that
we call InSTA, which is--

1106
00:50:57,932 --> 00:51:02,150
the idea here is, with
using Llama models,

1107
00:51:02,150 --> 00:51:06,630
to generate and verify
synthetic agentic tasks.

1108
00:51:06,630 --> 00:51:10,050
So we're thinking
about three stages.

1109
00:51:10,050 --> 00:51:12,993
There is a task
generation stage.

1110
00:51:12,993 --> 00:51:14,660
For example, I can
ask the model to say,

1111
00:51:14,660 --> 00:51:20,776
find me a code base for
generating images with Flux.1.

1112
00:51:20,776 --> 00:51:21,930
That's the first task.

1113
00:51:21,930 --> 00:51:23,805
I'll show you examples
of how we can do that.

1114
00:51:23,805 --> 00:51:25,850
The second task is evaluation.

1115
00:51:25,850 --> 00:51:28,920
Actually, executing this
task, and then trying

1116
00:51:28,920 --> 00:51:32,700
to see whether the
model solved the task.

1117
00:51:32,700 --> 00:51:36,660
And then the third step
is actually collection--

1118
00:51:36,660 --> 00:51:38,500
basically, collecting
it at scale.

1119
00:51:38,500 --> 00:51:40,050
But you can think
of this environment

1120
00:51:40,050 --> 00:51:41,950
is that the model
proposes tasks,

1121
00:51:41,950 --> 00:51:44,580
the model executes those
tasks, the model then evaluates

1122
00:51:44,580 --> 00:51:48,670
those tasks, and then if
it's successfully it says,

1123
00:51:48,670 --> 00:51:50,410
yeah, I propose the task.

1124
00:51:50,410 --> 00:51:53,410
I executed this task on
the real web, I evaluated,

1125
00:51:53,410 --> 00:51:55,120
I think I've solved the task.

1126
00:51:55,120 --> 00:52:01,350
Then that becomes a very useful
data for training our agents.

1127
00:52:01,350 --> 00:52:05,880
So let's see how we can do.

1128
00:52:05,880 --> 00:52:08,200
So, for example,
given the web domain,

1129
00:52:08,200 --> 00:52:11,580
for example, there's
a domain, just

1130
00:52:11,580 --> 00:52:16,020
random domain, Mersey Ferries,
it's a website in the UK,

1131
00:52:16,020 --> 00:52:18,480
and we can ask the
system to propose

1132
00:52:18,480 --> 00:52:20,430
realistic tasks that
an average user could

1133
00:52:20,430 --> 00:52:21,490
complete in one session.

1134
00:52:21,490 --> 00:52:22,907
So there's a certain
system prompt

1135
00:52:22,907 --> 00:52:26,300
that we're asking the
model to generate the task.

1136
00:52:26,300 --> 00:52:30,390
And what's interesting is that
if you look at Llama-70B-3.1,

1137
00:52:30,390 --> 00:52:32,640
I think we're also
training on Llama 3.3,

1138
00:52:32,640 --> 00:52:34,680
which is actually better,
has better results,

1139
00:52:34,680 --> 00:52:37,010
so these models
continue improving.

1140
00:52:37,010 --> 00:52:38,780
So if I ask the
model to describe,

1141
00:52:38,780 --> 00:52:41,700
what is this website about,
it's actually pretty good.

1142
00:52:41,700 --> 00:52:44,120
It says, well, this
particular website

1143
00:52:44,120 --> 00:52:47,300
is the official website for
famous Mersey Ferries service

1144
00:52:47,300 --> 00:52:49,380
in Liverpool,
England, and so forth.

1145
00:52:49,380 --> 00:52:50,670
Certain things you can do.

1146
00:52:50,670 --> 00:52:52,610
You can plan a journey.

1147
00:52:52,610 --> 00:52:57,110
You can book tickets
and gives you

1148
00:52:57,110 --> 00:53:00,350
ideas of what you can
do with this website.

1149
00:53:00,350 --> 00:53:02,720
And so that gave
us this notion of,

1150
00:53:02,720 --> 00:53:05,160
if the model understands
what this website is about,

1151
00:53:05,160 --> 00:53:08,660
maybe I can ask it to
actually generate the task.

1152
00:53:08,660 --> 00:53:12,740
And what we found is
that we can scale it

1153
00:53:12,740 --> 00:53:16,820
across many, many different
websites from Common Crawl.

1154
00:53:16,820 --> 00:53:19,590
I think we started with
about a million websites,

1155
00:53:19,590 --> 00:53:21,840
and we started thinking
about what kind of tasks

1156
00:53:21,840 --> 00:53:23,160
these models can generate.

1157
00:53:23,160 --> 00:53:26,590
And a lot of tasks
actually involve

1158
00:53:26,590 --> 00:53:29,090
some form of information
retrieval, some sort of search.

1159
00:53:29,090 --> 00:53:31,970
You're finding the right
ticket information.

1160
00:53:31,970 --> 00:53:35,420
You're finding
booking-- opening hours.

1161
00:53:35,420 --> 00:53:41,950
You find specific information
on a particular topic.

1162
00:53:41,950 --> 00:53:46,040
But crucially, we do not modify
the state of the internet.

1163
00:53:46,040 --> 00:53:49,870
So that was one of
the settings for us,

1164
00:53:49,870 --> 00:53:52,270
where we asking the
model to come up

1165
00:53:52,270 --> 00:53:55,580
with a task that don't modify
the state of the environment.

1166
00:53:55,580 --> 00:53:59,320
So, for example, booking
something, we can do.

1167
00:53:59,320 --> 00:54:02,500
Or clicking on something that
actually changes the state

1168
00:54:02,500 --> 00:54:05,410
of the environment, we can do.

1169
00:54:05,410 --> 00:54:09,670
This specifically was done to
avoid any sort of complications

1170
00:54:09,670 --> 00:54:10,850
of us being blocked.

1171
00:54:10,850 --> 00:54:13,030
And as I mentioned
before, we can't

1172
00:54:13,030 --> 00:54:17,698
go on united.com or
booking.com and start

1173
00:54:17,698 --> 00:54:21,820
running a lot of tasks, booking
a lot of hotels randomly.

1174
00:54:21,820 --> 00:54:23,650
So we can only navigate
to these websites,

1175
00:54:23,650 --> 00:54:29,220
but we actually cannot change
the state of the environment.

1176
00:54:29,220 --> 00:54:32,520
What we found is that a lot of
tasks that the models actually

1177
00:54:32,520 --> 00:54:35,640
come up with are
diverse, and many require

1178
00:54:35,640 --> 00:54:37,690
multiple steps of reasoning.

1179
00:54:37,690 --> 00:54:41,170
So, for example, if I
go to fonts.adobe.com,

1180
00:54:41,170 --> 00:54:43,200
some of the generated
task is browse fonts

1181
00:54:43,200 --> 00:54:45,368
suitable for a children's book.

1182
00:54:45,368 --> 00:54:47,160
So you have to understand
what makes a font

1183
00:54:47,160 --> 00:54:50,220
suitable for a children's book.

1184
00:54:50,220 --> 00:54:54,970
A lot of tasks can identify
facts that's likely to contain.

1185
00:54:54,970 --> 00:54:58,650
So, for example, if I'm
going to ancient-symbols.com,

1186
00:54:58,650 --> 00:55:02,100
the generated task would be,
look up the meaning of the word

1187
00:55:02,100 --> 00:55:06,756
Om symbol in ancient cultures.

1188
00:55:06,756 --> 00:55:09,403
You have to go on this
website, this is the task,

1189
00:55:09,403 --> 00:55:11,070
and I have to crawl
through this website

1190
00:55:11,070 --> 00:55:12,528
to actually find
what's the meaning

1191
00:55:12,528 --> 00:55:14,820
of that particular symbol.

1192
00:55:14,820 --> 00:55:23,170
Or, I can go to this particular
obscure Scottish design studio

1193
00:55:23,170 --> 00:55:26,310
and ask, view the
latest fabric designs

1194
00:55:26,310 --> 00:55:28,190
by this particular studio.

1195
00:55:28,190 --> 00:55:29,890
So I end up on
this website and I

1196
00:55:29,890 --> 00:55:33,880
have to find the latest
designs, fabric designs.

1197
00:55:33,880 --> 00:55:36,230
And this is how it looks like.

1198
00:55:36,230 --> 00:55:38,900
I actually have to end up
on this particular web page

1199
00:55:38,900 --> 00:55:41,800
because that lists the
latest fabric designs

1200
00:55:41,800 --> 00:55:44,000
by that particular studio.

1201
00:55:44,000 --> 00:55:47,140
So these tasks are
grounded even for sites

1202
00:55:47,140 --> 00:55:49,660
in the tail of the
data distributions.

1203
00:55:49,660 --> 00:55:53,660
So these are-- we wanted
to go for breadth,

1204
00:55:53,660 --> 00:55:59,020
not necessarily to only cover
a hundred most commonly visited

1205
00:55:59,020 --> 00:56:01,630
websites, but also
going to these--

1206
00:56:01,630 --> 00:56:05,410
which gives us the diversity
of the environments

1207
00:56:05,410 --> 00:56:11,680
and different websites and
different generated tasks.

1208
00:56:11,680 --> 00:56:18,990
So that's the first step,
try to generate the tasks.

1209
00:56:18,990 --> 00:56:24,000
Now, the second
state is, OK, how

1210
00:56:24,000 --> 00:56:26,850
do we know when these
tasks are solved?

1211
00:56:26,850 --> 00:56:30,100
And that gives us the
task evaluation part.

1212
00:56:30,100 --> 00:56:32,340
And the way we do
this is we basically

1213
00:56:32,340 --> 00:56:36,850
say, OK, if we're given
the task, generate a task,

1214
00:56:36,850 --> 00:56:39,940
we can execute this task
using the current model.

1215
00:56:39,940 --> 00:56:43,690
Let's say you have a GPT model
or you have your Llama model,

1216
00:56:43,690 --> 00:56:46,900
in this case, it was a Llama
model that we're using,

1217
00:56:46,900 --> 00:56:48,720
you observe a
sequence of actions

1218
00:56:48,720 --> 00:56:51,360
and the last
observation, and you now

1219
00:56:51,360 --> 00:56:54,640
estimate the probability
of a task being successful.

1220
00:56:54,640 --> 00:56:56,910
So you're basically
asking the model--

1221
00:56:56,910 --> 00:57:00,070
given the last state in
the sequence of actions,

1222
00:57:00,070 --> 00:57:03,340
you're asking it to estimate
the probability of the success.

1223
00:57:03,340 --> 00:57:06,270
This is, again, the same
idea as using large language

1224
00:57:06,270 --> 00:57:08,070
model as a judge.

1225
00:57:08,070 --> 00:57:11,080
Here we're actually evaluating
the full trajectory,

1226
00:57:11,080 --> 00:57:13,020
but you can think of it as--

1227
00:57:13,020 --> 00:57:16,180
in the previous part when we
were looking at the search,

1228
00:57:16,180 --> 00:57:18,310
we essentially we're
using the same piece,

1229
00:57:18,310 --> 00:57:20,850
but only evaluating
partial trajectories

1230
00:57:20,850 --> 00:57:22,850
to try to figure out
what we should explore

1231
00:57:22,850 --> 00:57:24,100
and what we shouldn't explore.

1232
00:57:24,100 --> 00:57:26,800
Here we're actually trying to
evaluate the full trajectory.

1233
00:57:26,800 --> 00:57:29,360
But, obviously, you can combine
search with this technique

1234
00:57:29,360 --> 00:57:29,860
as well.

1235
00:57:29,860 --> 00:57:35,290
So you can search around and
try to find the best outcome.

1236
00:57:35,290 --> 00:57:37,710
And you prompt Llama to rate
the confidence of the tasks

1237
00:57:37,710 --> 00:57:40,890
it solved from 0 to 1.

1238
00:57:40,890 --> 00:57:48,270
And what we found is that, for
example, out of 150,000 tasks,

1239
00:57:48,270 --> 00:57:53,580
about 15% are rated as success
with high confidence by Llama,

1240
00:57:53,580 --> 00:57:55,660
roughly 22,000 tasks.

1241
00:57:55,660 --> 00:58:01,570
We actually did verify how
good using Llama as a judge.

1242
00:58:01,570 --> 00:58:07,570
And I think that in terms of
the success, it was about 90%.

1243
00:58:07,570 --> 00:58:10,270
So, for example,
when the model says,

1244
00:58:10,270 --> 00:58:13,930
I'm pretty sure I've solved the
task, with human evaluations,

1245
00:58:13,930 --> 00:58:17,843
we're seeing close to 90%
accuracy among those ones.

1246
00:58:17,843 --> 00:58:19,260
So basically means,
whenever Llama

1247
00:58:19,260 --> 00:58:22,170
says, yes, I've solved
the task, 90% of the time,

1248
00:58:22,170 --> 00:58:24,302
it actually solved the
task, 10% of the time,

1249
00:58:24,302 --> 00:58:25,510
no, it didn't solve the task.

1250
00:58:25,510 --> 00:58:28,330
So these verifiers
are not perfect,

1251
00:58:28,330 --> 00:58:29,850
but they are pretty
decent in terms

1252
00:58:29,850 --> 00:58:32,580
of approximately telling you
whether you solved the task

1253
00:58:32,580 --> 00:58:34,230
or not.

1254
00:58:34,230 --> 00:58:41,640
So that gives you this notion of
showing that you've successfully

1255
00:58:41,640 --> 00:58:44,940
found the task, executed,
and then verified it.

1256
00:58:44,940 --> 00:58:47,730
Let me give you a
visual representation

1257
00:58:47,730 --> 00:58:50,490
of what that looks like.

1258
00:58:50,490 --> 00:58:53,190
So on a different
project, we have a project

1259
00:58:53,190 --> 00:58:54,960
where we're working
with ecologists

1260
00:58:54,960 --> 00:59:01,860
on identifying invasive plants
in different environments.

1261
00:59:01,860 --> 00:59:06,060
And we basically
thought, OK, let's

1262
00:59:06,060 --> 00:59:09,600
look at this website,
Invasive Plants Atlas,

1263
00:59:09,600 --> 00:59:12,810
and prompt the model
to generate a task

1264
00:59:12,810 --> 00:59:14,520
and see if the model
can solve the task.

1265
00:59:14,520 --> 00:59:16,187
So the model basically
generates a task,

1266
00:59:16,187 --> 00:59:18,770
find invasive plant species
native to North America.

1267
00:59:18,770 --> 00:59:21,720
So that's more like
information extraction.

1268
00:59:21,720 --> 00:59:25,280
And the agent response is to
find an invasive plant species

1269
00:59:25,280 --> 00:59:26,010
in North America.

1270
00:59:26,010 --> 00:59:28,700
I will first click
on link all species

1271
00:59:28,700 --> 00:59:30,420
to view the list of all species.

1272
00:59:30,420 --> 00:59:31,172
OK.

1273
00:59:31,172 --> 00:59:33,860
So it takes and, based
on this information,

1274
00:59:33,860 --> 00:59:35,640
it clicks on All Species.

1275
00:59:35,640 --> 00:59:37,590
And that's what the
action looks like.

1276
00:59:37,590 --> 00:59:41,410
It's a little JSON that you
have an action key click,

1277
00:59:41,410 --> 00:59:44,060
you have action
arguments that can go in,

1278
00:59:44,060 --> 00:59:48,050
and you have a target element
ID, the ID that you associated

1279
00:59:48,050 --> 00:59:50,990
with that particular click.

1280
00:59:50,990 --> 00:59:53,480
Then it basically says,
to find invasive plant

1281
00:59:53,480 --> 00:59:56,390
species native to
North America, I

1282
00:59:56,390 --> 00:59:58,910
will first click
on the link home

1283
00:59:58,910 --> 01:00:01,100
because it's trying
to go to the sources.

1284
01:00:01,100 --> 01:00:08,752
And it sort of misses the point
because there is a sources here.

1285
01:00:08,752 --> 01:00:10,730
But it actually
insists on going home

1286
01:00:10,730 --> 01:00:12,060
and then to find the sources.

1287
01:00:12,060 --> 01:00:16,235
So it misread, so it made
a mistake in this case.

1288
01:00:16,235 --> 01:00:18,610
Since the link to sources is
not on the current web page,

1289
01:00:18,610 --> 01:00:20,450
but it is, so it's
making a mistake,

1290
01:00:20,450 --> 01:00:23,420
I will first click on the Home
and go back to the main page.

1291
01:00:23,420 --> 01:00:25,310
So it goes back
to the main page,

1292
01:00:25,310 --> 01:00:29,000
and then it identifies
the sources.

1293
01:00:29,000 --> 01:00:33,010
So it clicks on the Sources.

1294
01:00:33,010 --> 01:00:35,890
It actually gets
to the right piece,

1295
01:00:35,890 --> 01:00:38,480
to find invasive plants
native to North America,

1296
01:00:38,480 --> 01:00:41,800
I will first click on the
A under the native range

1297
01:00:41,800 --> 01:00:42,980
taxonomic references.

1298
01:00:42,980 --> 01:00:48,110
So it execute pieces, and
it does clicks on that,

1299
01:00:48,110 --> 01:00:50,060
on the correct element.

1300
01:00:50,060 --> 01:00:53,200
And then once it gets
to the correct element,

1301
01:00:53,200 --> 01:00:59,800
it then basically says, I
need to sort based on US--

1302
01:00:59,800 --> 01:01:03,790
plants that are native to the
US, gets it in a JSON format,

1303
01:01:03,790 --> 01:01:06,940
and then gives you the answer.

1304
01:01:06,940 --> 01:01:09,160
Since the list is already
sorted by US nativity

1305
01:01:09,160 --> 01:01:10,670
by ascending order, that's it.

1306
01:01:10,670 --> 01:01:13,980
I can find the first like
165 plant species that

1307
01:01:13,980 --> 01:01:15,510
are native to North America.

1308
01:01:15,510 --> 01:01:17,440
So this gives you the list.

1309
01:01:17,440 --> 01:01:19,770
And the answer is, basically,
I give you the web page,

1310
01:01:19,770 --> 01:01:22,335
and I tell you, the first
165 plant species on the list

1311
01:01:22,335 --> 01:01:24,460
are invasive plant species
native to North America.

1312
01:01:24,460 --> 01:01:28,660
So in terms of me finding
information seeking,

1313
01:01:28,660 --> 01:01:32,670
again, this was a completely
synthetically generated task

1314
01:01:32,670 --> 01:01:35,830
where the model
proposed the task.

1315
01:01:35,830 --> 01:01:40,260
It's now executed the task,
even though it made a mistake,

1316
01:01:40,260 --> 01:01:42,700
and then it gave you the action.

1317
01:01:42,700 --> 01:01:47,432
And in this case, despite
reasoning, some of the reasoning

1318
01:01:47,432 --> 01:01:49,390
failures, because it
clicked on the wrong thing

1319
01:01:49,390 --> 01:01:53,320
but then it recovered, it
found the target information,

1320
01:01:53,320 --> 01:01:55,830
and in this case, Llama verifier
evaluates this trajectory

1321
01:01:55,830 --> 01:01:58,112
successfully with
confidence 1.0.

1322
01:01:58,112 --> 01:02:00,670
And, again, whenever it
does this verification,

1323
01:02:00,670 --> 01:02:02,380
it's about 90% accurate.

1324
01:02:02,380 --> 01:02:04,470
Maybe close to-- maybe
85%, 90% accurate

1325
01:02:04,470 --> 01:02:09,130
when we did the
studies, human studies.

1326
01:02:09,130 --> 01:02:15,985
So that gives you an interesting
synthetic generation.

1327
01:02:15,985 --> 01:02:19,510
Where you're proposing the task,
you're executing this task,

1328
01:02:19,510 --> 01:02:22,010
and then you verify, did
you solve the task or not?

1329
01:02:22,010 --> 01:02:25,720
And, again, only 14%
or 15% of the tasks

1330
01:02:25,720 --> 01:02:29,510
that the model proposed and
executes, only 15% of them,

1331
01:02:29,510 --> 01:02:32,440
the model basically says, yes,
I think I solved the task.

1332
01:02:32,440 --> 01:02:35,330
85% of them, you
can't solve the task.

1333
01:02:35,330 --> 01:02:38,810
But that's OK because it's
all done automatically.

1334
01:02:38,810 --> 01:02:41,800
There is no human
in the loop here.

1335
01:02:41,800 --> 01:02:43,840
Let me show you some
examples, other examples

1336
01:02:43,840 --> 01:02:45,110
of what the model does.

1337
01:02:45,110 --> 01:02:46,430
So here's the example.

1338
01:02:46,430 --> 01:02:49,160
Find me the opening hours
of La Sagrada Familia.

1339
01:02:49,160 --> 01:02:52,010
It's a fairly famous
church in Barcelona.

1340
01:02:52,010 --> 01:02:57,820
So it proposes a task,
it browses the web,

1341
01:02:57,820 --> 01:03:00,895
and it finds the answer.

1342
01:03:00,895 --> 01:03:04,040
So, again, this is the
task that it proposes.

1343
01:03:04,040 --> 01:03:05,420
It goes to the website.

1344
01:03:05,420 --> 01:03:08,980
It actually finds
the Sagrada Familia,

1345
01:03:08,980 --> 01:03:11,850
it clicks, it finds, and it
basically, from the table,

1346
01:03:11,850 --> 01:03:13,243
finds what the right answer is.

1347
01:03:13,243 --> 01:03:15,660
Well, find the information on
the European Union's climate

1348
01:03:15,660 --> 01:03:17,080
action policies.

1349
01:03:17,080 --> 01:03:20,550
So it goes to the European Union
Commission, it crawls the web,

1350
01:03:20,550 --> 01:03:23,160
and then this is a
success where it actually

1351
01:03:23,160 --> 01:03:28,290
finds the documents related
to the climate action progress

1352
01:03:28,290 --> 01:03:29,980
or climate action policies.

1353
01:03:29,980 --> 01:03:36,240
And, again, this tasks are
completely created by model

1354
01:03:36,240 --> 01:03:40,230
proposing what the task should
be, model executing this task,

1355
01:03:40,230 --> 01:03:43,380
and then the model verifying
whether it successfully

1356
01:03:43,380 --> 01:03:44,650
completed the task or not.

1357
01:03:44,650 --> 01:03:47,320
So we went through
these two stages.

1358
01:03:47,320 --> 01:03:51,160
And, again, I'm not saying
that that's a perfect setting.

1359
01:03:51,160 --> 01:03:54,700
I think that if you
incorporate search,

1360
01:03:54,700 --> 01:03:58,950
if you have better models,
and then you iterate it

1361
01:03:58,950 --> 01:04:02,670
multiple times, I think
the quality of generation

1362
01:04:02,670 --> 01:04:06,690
of these tasks could
improve substantially.

1363
01:04:06,690 --> 01:04:09,480
So let me talk a little bit
about the data collection.

1364
01:04:09,480 --> 01:04:12,090
So what happens with
the data collection?

1365
01:04:12,090 --> 01:04:15,110
Now we can scale up
the data collection.

1366
01:04:15,110 --> 01:04:17,990
So we've used the
Common Crawl PageRank

1367
01:04:17,990 --> 01:04:19,800
to find the most
important websites.

1368
01:04:19,800 --> 01:04:24,170
We start with the top
1 million web pages.

1369
01:04:24,170 --> 01:04:29,630
We filtered it down to
about 150,000 live websites

1370
01:04:29,630 --> 01:04:31,928
by filtering harmful content.

1371
01:04:31,928 --> 01:04:33,470
So we're prompting
the model so if we

1372
01:04:33,470 --> 01:04:36,330
think that there is harmful
content on the website,

1373
01:04:36,330 --> 01:04:38,000
we basically ignore it.

1374
01:04:38,000 --> 01:04:40,310
So we're about 97%,
actually, pretty high,

1375
01:04:40,310 --> 01:04:42,690
in terms of detecting and
filtering harmful content.

1376
01:04:42,690 --> 01:04:44,820
This is used using Llama models.

1377
01:04:44,820 --> 01:04:48,650
We are about 89% success
rate in generating

1378
01:04:48,650 --> 01:04:52,250
feasible tasks, and about
80%, so it's like 90%,

1379
01:04:52,250 --> 01:04:54,380
in generating feasible
tasks, which basically means

1380
01:04:54,380 --> 01:04:59,900
that when we're verifying
whether the task is successful

1381
01:04:59,900 --> 01:05:02,510
or not, we're 89%.

1382
01:05:02,510 --> 01:05:05,750
And 82% accuracy in judging
successful task completion.

1383
01:05:05,750 --> 01:05:07,000
Sorry, that's the one where--

1384
01:05:07,000 --> 01:05:10,815
82% whether the task is
successfully completed or not.

1385
01:05:10,815 --> 01:05:13,394

1386
01:05:13,394 --> 01:05:18,080
And, again, we went through
150,000 live websites,

1387
01:05:18,080 --> 01:05:23,140
and then we attempting it and
verifying it and creating data

1388
01:05:23,140 --> 01:05:26,050
based on whether we successfully
generate the task and whether we

1389
01:05:26,050 --> 01:05:29,200
successfully execute it
and then verified success.

1390
01:05:29,200 --> 01:05:33,370
What's interesting is that we
found that on toy-ish examples

1391
01:05:33,370 --> 01:05:35,300
and small examples,
Mind2Web, WebLINX.

1392
01:05:35,300 --> 01:05:40,100
These are some of the
data sets that people use.

1393
01:05:40,100 --> 01:05:42,310
We found that
training on synthetic

1394
01:05:42,310 --> 01:05:44,620
and human demonstration scale
faster than just training

1395
01:05:44,620 --> 01:05:46,960
on human demonstrations.

1396
01:05:46,960 --> 01:05:50,950
So actually, if you're mixing
what humans collected versus

1397
01:05:50,950 --> 01:05:55,250
if you're mixing synthetic and
humans, we can see improvements.

1398
01:05:55,250 --> 01:05:57,550
We also see that
adding synthetic data

1399
01:05:57,550 --> 01:06:05,400
improves step accuracy by
certain numbers, so that's good.

1400
01:06:05,400 --> 01:06:10,550
We also found that if you
only use human annotations,

1401
01:06:10,550 --> 01:06:13,290
human annotations do
struggle with generalization.

1402
01:06:13,290 --> 01:06:15,560
Because a lot of times what
actually ends up happening

1403
01:06:15,560 --> 01:06:17,185
is that with human
annotations, they're

1404
01:06:17,185 --> 01:06:23,060
typically done on a small subset
of highly-visited websites.

1405
01:06:23,060 --> 01:06:27,870
And the ability to generalize
to the tail of the distribution,

1406
01:06:27,870 --> 01:06:30,890
that's where synthetic data
really helps us because it can

1407
01:06:30,890 --> 01:06:34,340
help us generalize to the tail
of the distribution, where

1408
01:06:34,340 --> 01:06:37,800
it's very difficult for
humans to collect the data.

1409
01:06:37,800 --> 01:06:39,890
Obviously, the
ultimate choice would

1410
01:06:39,890 --> 01:06:43,020
be to get humans to
annotate the entire web.

1411
01:06:43,020 --> 01:06:45,110
That would be the
best because then we

1412
01:06:45,110 --> 01:06:47,240
can just do imitation learning.

1413
01:06:47,240 --> 01:06:51,200
But we do find that, the
ability to crawl the web

1414
01:06:51,200 --> 01:06:53,220
and look at this
tail distribution,

1415
01:06:53,220 --> 01:06:57,230
we do see improvements
in generalization.

1416
01:06:57,230 --> 01:07:01,490
Now, there are 385
million unique domains,

1417
01:07:01,490 --> 01:07:04,330
suggesting maybe another
1,000x more data that could be

1418
01:07:04,330 --> 01:07:05,990
available by scaling further.

1419
01:07:05,990 --> 01:07:09,100
We were constrained by
compute and academic resources

1420
01:07:09,100 --> 01:07:10,690
when we did this work.

1421
01:07:10,690 --> 01:07:13,030
And, of course, there
is also this notion

1422
01:07:13,030 --> 01:07:15,470
of moving towards online
reinforcement learning.

1423
01:07:15,470 --> 01:07:19,730
So you can think about settings
where you run this procedure.

1424
01:07:19,730 --> 01:07:23,170
You collect the synthetic data,
and then you update the model

1425
01:07:23,170 --> 01:07:23,990
and do it again.

1426
01:07:23,990 --> 01:07:28,190
So you can do it
in online fashion.

1427
01:07:28,190 --> 01:07:30,350
You crawl the web, you
generate these tasks,

1428
01:07:30,350 --> 01:07:32,845
you try to solve
some of these tasks,

1429
01:07:32,845 --> 01:07:34,970
you update the model
parameters, and do that again.

1430
01:07:34,970 --> 01:07:36,195
So if you have an online--

1431
01:07:36,195 --> 01:07:38,320
and that could be an online
reinforcement learning,

1432
01:07:38,320 --> 01:07:41,240
but done at scale, at
the internet scale,

1433
01:07:41,240 --> 01:07:44,780
I think these models would
improve significantly.

1434
01:07:44,780 --> 01:07:46,570
And, of course,
it's very difficult

1435
01:07:46,570 --> 01:07:49,210
to do that on the
real web because it's

1436
01:07:49,210 --> 01:07:51,320
very hard to change--

1437
01:07:51,320 --> 01:07:54,550
you can't change the
state of the environment.

1438
01:07:54,550 --> 01:08:01,320
So you have to be careful how
you're actually scaling it up

1439
01:08:01,320 --> 01:08:05,430
when you go to the real
world or the real web.

1440
01:08:05,430 --> 01:08:07,890
Let me just, in the
last 10 minutes and I'll

1441
01:08:07,890 --> 01:08:09,960
leave the room for
questions, I wanted

1442
01:08:09,960 --> 01:08:15,900
to point out that this
notion of agentic environment

1443
01:08:15,900 --> 01:08:19,380
is also applicable to
the physical agents

1444
01:08:19,380 --> 01:08:22,120
or robots in general, right?

1445
01:08:22,120 --> 01:08:24,790
So we've also-- very
parallel effort,

1446
01:08:24,790 --> 01:08:28,950
we basically thought,
OK, these AI agents have

1447
01:08:28,950 --> 01:08:32,502
the same notion whether
we're operating on the web

1448
01:08:32,502 --> 01:08:34,710
and taking actions on the
web, or whether we actually

1449
01:08:34,710 --> 01:08:37,979
have a physical agent, like
a robot that takes actions

1450
01:08:37,979 --> 01:08:39,220
in the real world, right?

1451
01:08:39,220 --> 01:08:41,939
So, for example, you can
think about observing

1452
01:08:41,939 --> 01:08:44,500
the data, which could be the
image plus maybe the depths.

1453
01:08:44,500 --> 01:08:46,350
You do some form of
semantic segmentation

1454
01:08:46,350 --> 01:08:47,710
to understand the environment.

1455
01:08:47,710 --> 01:08:48,670
You have an objective.

1456
01:08:48,670 --> 01:08:50,490
You generate a
high-level plan and then

1457
01:08:50,490 --> 01:08:52,050
you generate the
low-level actions

1458
01:08:52,050 --> 01:08:54,430
like interacting with
the actual environment.

1459
01:08:54,430 --> 01:08:58,630
So we have this work on
planning learning, what

1460
01:08:58,630 --> 01:09:01,180
we call plan, sequence, and
learn, where the idea is

1461
01:09:01,180 --> 01:09:03,189
a language model
basically gives you

1462
01:09:03,189 --> 01:09:06,920
a sequence of high-level
plan, what you need to do.

1463
01:09:06,920 --> 01:09:09,069
You have a sequencing
or parsing module

1464
01:09:09,069 --> 01:09:12,470
that basically understands the
environment, sequences this.

1465
01:09:12,470 --> 01:09:14,258
And then we have a
low-level action,

1466
01:09:14,258 --> 01:09:16,300
which we learn with
reinforcement learning, which

1467
01:09:16,300 --> 01:09:18,160
is a little policy that
actually learns how

1468
01:09:18,160 --> 01:09:19,609
to take a particular action.

1469
01:09:19,609 --> 01:09:22,930
So very much in
flavor, very similar

1470
01:09:22,930 --> 01:09:25,100
to what the web
agents are doing,

1471
01:09:25,100 --> 01:09:27,500
but now we're actually
interacting in the real world.

1472
01:09:27,500 --> 01:09:31,750
So, for example, a planning
module can say, OK, here's

1473
01:09:31,750 --> 01:09:33,562
the task.

1474
01:09:33,562 --> 01:09:37,630
Get me-- I'm trying to put the
silver nut on the silver peg

1475
01:09:37,630 --> 01:09:39,253
and the gold nut
on the gold peg,

1476
01:09:39,253 --> 01:09:41,420
and then give me the simple
plan to solve this task.

1477
01:09:41,420 --> 01:09:45,250
And so the LLM basically
produces a fairly simple task

1478
01:09:45,250 --> 01:09:47,660
of how I should solve it.

1479
01:09:47,660 --> 01:09:52,520
We have a sequencing model,
which looks at the observation.

1480
01:09:52,520 --> 01:09:54,189
There's a depth.

1481
01:09:54,189 --> 01:09:58,270
We do segmentation and a
little bit of projections,

1482
01:09:58,270 --> 01:10:00,330
so have some form
of 3D representation

1483
01:10:00,330 --> 01:10:01,590
of the environment.

1484
01:10:01,590 --> 01:10:03,752
And then it goes through
the inverse kinematic,

1485
01:10:03,752 --> 01:10:04,960
and we have a motion planner.

1486
01:10:04,960 --> 01:10:06,377
But you can think
of this as like,

1487
01:10:06,377 --> 01:10:08,670
again, understanding
the environment

1488
01:10:08,670 --> 01:10:12,100
and doing a little bit
of motion planning.

1489
01:10:12,100 --> 01:10:15,270
And then the low-level
action module actually

1490
01:10:15,270 --> 01:10:16,570
learns the local policy.

1491
01:10:16,570 --> 01:10:19,740
So here you have reinforcement
learning for interactions.

1492
01:10:19,740 --> 01:10:21,990
Kind of locally learning
how to interact with it.

1493
01:10:21,990 --> 01:10:24,400
Much like when you're clicking
on a particular element.

1494
01:10:24,400 --> 01:10:28,230
Here we're actually learning how
to control our joint angles so

1495
01:10:28,230 --> 01:10:32,110
that we can accomplish
a low-level task.

1496
01:10:32,110 --> 01:10:33,970
And so this is
how it looks like,

1497
01:10:33,970 --> 01:10:37,235
where a local model is
actually learning how to--

1498
01:10:37,235 --> 01:10:38,610
with reinforcement
learning, it's

1499
01:10:38,610 --> 01:10:41,830
actually learning how to
interact with the environment.

1500
01:10:41,830 --> 01:10:45,820
And so in the full pipeline,
very much in similar spirit,

1501
01:10:45,820 --> 01:10:50,700
you have a planning
model that plans high--

1502
01:10:50,700 --> 01:10:52,670
gives you a high-level plan.

1503
01:10:52,670 --> 01:10:55,460
For each high-level
plan, you're executing

1504
01:10:55,460 --> 01:10:58,670
this lower-level actions
where you basically

1505
01:10:58,670 --> 01:11:01,430
have pose estimation, motion
planning, a little bit

1506
01:11:01,430 --> 01:11:03,930
of reinforcement learning
to take the action.

1507
01:11:03,930 --> 01:11:08,450
And then you have this low-level
control that actually controls

1508
01:11:08,450 --> 01:11:10,530
low-level actions via
reinforcement learning,

1509
01:11:10,530 --> 01:11:11,430
and then you proceed.

1510
01:11:11,430 --> 01:11:13,410
And that's just the first
step, and you go to the next,

1511
01:11:13,410 --> 01:11:15,800
and then you go to the next,
and execute that according

1512
01:11:15,800 --> 01:11:19,460
to the plan, and so forth.

1513
01:11:19,460 --> 01:11:24,840
And we found that these sort
of models work reasonably well.

1514
01:11:24,840 --> 01:11:28,520
PSL solves about 25
long-horizon robotic tasks

1515
01:11:28,520 --> 01:11:33,590
across, in this case, four
benchmarks, with about 85%

1516
01:11:33,590 --> 01:11:34,760
success rate.

1517
01:11:34,760 --> 01:11:41,370
We also tried extending this
to the actual real environment.

1518
01:11:41,370 --> 01:11:43,010
So here is the--

1519
01:11:43,010 --> 01:11:45,180
I give it a task, put
mouse in the drawer.

1520
01:11:45,180 --> 01:11:47,730
So the model basically
generates the plan,

1521
01:11:47,730 --> 01:11:49,410
and, again, it looks
at the environment,

1522
01:11:49,410 --> 01:11:53,444
parses the environment, and
then executes low-level actions.

1523
01:11:53,444 --> 01:11:59,570
And it does a lot of
interesting things.

1524
01:11:59,570 --> 01:12:01,540
But the point I do
want to make here

1525
01:12:01,540 --> 01:12:04,990
is that, whether you're
operating in a virtual space

1526
01:12:04,990 --> 01:12:09,280
or whether you are operating
in the real-world space,

1527
01:12:09,280 --> 01:12:14,030
the ideas are fairly
similar across domains.

1528
01:12:14,030 --> 01:12:17,290
And so here, you can generalize
to new object, object

1529
01:12:17,290 --> 01:12:18,980
geometries, new categories.

1530
01:12:18,980 --> 01:12:23,435
And so we've never trained
on this particular--

1531
01:12:23,435 --> 01:12:25,000
for this particular
object, but you

1532
01:12:25,000 --> 01:12:27,440
can successfully complete it.

1533
01:12:27,440 --> 01:12:32,360
You can deal with multiple novel
objects with unseen receptacles.

1534
01:12:32,360 --> 01:12:39,710
So these models can
do reasonably well.

1535
01:12:39,710 --> 01:12:41,390
It's not perfect, by all means.

1536
01:12:41,390 --> 01:12:46,180
I think we're hitting success
rates about 80% to 90%.

1537
01:12:46,180 --> 01:12:47,720
But these models do generalize.

1538
01:12:47,720 --> 01:12:49,640
They can manipulate
deformable objects.

1539
01:12:49,640 --> 01:12:52,400
You don't observe them when
we're training these models

1540
01:12:52,400 --> 01:12:54,590
in the simulator, but
training in the simulator

1541
01:12:54,590 --> 01:12:57,650
does translate into
the real world.

1542
01:12:57,650 --> 01:12:58,430
OK.

1543
01:12:58,430 --> 01:13:02,570
Now, to summarize, we've
talked about VisualWebArena

1544
01:13:02,570 --> 01:13:06,440
as a benchmark for these
realistic tasks designed

1545
01:13:06,440 --> 01:13:09,870
to evaluate the capabilities of
autonomous multimodal agents.

1546
01:13:09,870 --> 01:13:13,080
We've talked about inference
time search algorithm,

1547
01:13:13,080 --> 01:13:17,600
which is designed to enhance the
capabilities of language models

1548
01:13:17,600 --> 01:13:22,440
for realistic web tasks, and
how we can do the search.

1549
01:13:22,440 --> 01:13:24,710
And there's a lot
of research that

1550
01:13:24,710 --> 01:13:26,190
needs to be done in that space.

1551
01:13:26,190 --> 01:13:28,730
And then we've talked
about the data pipeline

1552
01:13:28,730 --> 01:13:30,740
for large-scale generation
and verification

1553
01:13:30,740 --> 01:13:33,590
of these synthetic
tasks that the model can

1554
01:13:33,590 --> 01:13:36,540
create powered by Llama models.

1555
01:13:36,540 --> 01:13:39,080
And the interesting thing
about all of these settings

1556
01:13:39,080 --> 01:13:42,210
is that you can try to
generate these synthetic tasks.

1557
01:13:42,210 --> 01:13:45,210
You can use inference time
to generate something better.

1558
01:13:45,210 --> 01:13:46,990
You can use data
collection with humans,

1559
01:13:46,990 --> 01:13:48,490
and ultimately,
it's the combination

1560
01:13:48,490 --> 01:13:51,760
of all of these pieces
that will potentially

1561
01:13:51,760 --> 01:13:55,720
lead to models that
can work very well

1562
01:13:55,720 --> 01:13:57,880
in the real-world environments.

1563
01:13:57,880 --> 01:14:00,220
One last thing I
do want to mention

1564
01:14:00,220 --> 01:14:04,900
is that, AI safety
and robustness

1565
01:14:04,900 --> 01:14:07,240
becomes fairly
important, especially

1566
01:14:07,240 --> 01:14:09,140
in the age of
autonomous systems.

1567
01:14:09,140 --> 01:14:11,050
And what's essentially
happening today

1568
01:14:11,050 --> 01:14:14,510
is that these systems
are not-- first of all,

1569
01:14:14,510 --> 01:14:17,170
they're not very robust.

1570
01:14:17,170 --> 01:14:21,975
If I ask it to do some task,
sometimes it completes the task,

1571
01:14:21,975 --> 01:14:22,850
sometimes it doesn't.

1572
01:14:22,850 --> 01:14:25,630
And it's very
difficult-- like, if I

1573
01:14:25,630 --> 01:14:29,145
want to have a system that
books my flights as an example,

1574
01:14:29,145 --> 01:14:32,300
I don't want to have a system
that's only 80% correct.

1575
01:14:32,300 --> 01:14:37,120
I don't want a system that 80%
books the right stuff and 20%

1576
01:14:37,120 --> 01:14:39,560
doesn't book the right--
books the wrong planes for me.

1577
01:14:39,560 --> 01:14:41,320
That's unacceptable.

1578
01:14:41,320 --> 01:14:44,290
So having these agentic
capabilities that actually

1579
01:14:44,290 --> 01:14:47,100
are very good at executing
tasks and perhaps

1580
01:14:47,100 --> 01:14:48,690
asking for
clarification questions

1581
01:14:48,690 --> 01:14:51,270
so you have some model
in the loop becomes

1582
01:14:51,270 --> 01:14:55,732
very important research and very
important, which is missing--

1583
01:14:55,732 --> 01:14:56,940
I think that's missing today.

1584
01:14:56,940 --> 01:15:00,280
On AI safety, that's
another area of research.

1585
01:15:00,280 --> 01:15:03,100
Because a lot of times, these
agents are very brittle.

1586
01:15:03,100 --> 01:15:08,350
So you can actually
adversely modify them.

1587
01:15:08,350 --> 01:15:10,530
And so this is some work.

1588
01:15:10,530 --> 01:15:13,620
There's a lot more work that's
happening in this space.

1589
01:15:13,620 --> 01:15:16,000
But we found this one
interesting settings,

1590
01:15:16,000 --> 01:15:21,090
this was done on VisualWebArena,
where we basically change

1591
01:15:21,090 --> 01:15:23,140
the representation of images.

1592
01:15:23,140 --> 01:15:25,930
So you have a web page, on the
web page, you have your images,

1593
01:15:25,930 --> 01:15:27,810
and you change
your representation

1594
01:15:27,810 --> 01:15:31,080
of the images in such a way
that the captioning system, when

1595
01:15:31,080 --> 01:15:35,160
it captions it, actually
provides an instruction

1596
01:15:35,160 --> 01:15:37,972
to the language model of
what it should do next.

1597
01:15:37,972 --> 01:15:43,110
So we can hijack these
agents and basically say--

1598
01:15:43,110 --> 01:15:45,350
add the comment, looks
great, before providing

1599
01:15:45,350 --> 01:15:46,170
the next action.

1600
01:15:46,170 --> 01:15:49,130
So if the model ends
up on your web page,

1601
01:15:49,130 --> 01:15:51,390
instead of navigating
to the next one,

1602
01:15:51,390 --> 01:15:55,057
it will leave the
comment saying,

1603
01:15:55,057 --> 01:15:57,390
this is a terrific product,
and then go to the next one.

1604
01:15:57,390 --> 01:16:04,490
So you can force it to
generate, for example,

1605
01:16:04,490 --> 01:16:07,910
positive feedback for your
product so you can boost your--

1606
01:16:07,910 --> 01:16:11,630

1607
01:16:11,630 --> 01:16:14,370
a lot of agents or
people like your product.

1608
01:16:14,370 --> 01:16:17,330
So it's just it's a little
bit toy-ish at this point,

1609
01:16:17,330 --> 01:16:19,847
but it becomes fairly
important because you

1610
01:16:19,847 --> 01:16:22,430
don't want to have a system--
you want to have a system that's

1611
01:16:22,430 --> 01:16:24,680
robust to these
adversarial settings

1612
01:16:24,680 --> 01:16:27,770
where I can modify my
state in an adversarial way

1613
01:16:27,770 --> 01:16:32,900
so as to ask the model to take
a different action than it's

1614
01:16:32,900 --> 01:16:35,060
supposed to take.

1615
01:16:35,060 --> 01:16:37,110
And finally, this
is a funny example,

1616
01:16:37,110 --> 01:16:39,830
but I wanted to
mention, like, there's

1617
01:16:39,830 --> 01:16:42,630
a lot of work in the industry on
building these types of models.

1618
01:16:42,630 --> 01:16:45,780
Obviously, we want to have
systems that can look at the web

1619
01:16:45,780 --> 01:16:48,720
and do a lot of tasks on you.

1620
01:16:48,720 --> 01:16:54,780
But this was done by Claude
Anthropic's computer use.

1621
01:16:54,780 --> 01:16:58,170
And so it kind of-- they have
this funny thing where you say,

1622
01:16:58,170 --> 01:17:00,960
in one episode,
Claude accidentally

1623
01:17:00,960 --> 01:17:04,150
clicked to stop a long
running screen recording,

1624
01:17:04,150 --> 01:17:06,870
causing all footage to be lost,
and another clock suddenly

1625
01:17:06,870 --> 01:17:08,460
took a break from
our coding demo

1626
01:17:08,460 --> 01:17:12,070
and began to use photos of
Yellowstone National Park.

1627
01:17:12,070 --> 01:17:14,230
And the reason why
these models do that?

1628
01:17:14,230 --> 01:17:16,810
Because you have a sequence,
you have a set of actions.

1629
01:17:16,810 --> 01:17:19,080
And so if it's trying
to accomplish the task,

1630
01:17:19,080 --> 01:17:22,020
takes the wrong
action, then it--

1631
01:17:22,020 --> 01:17:23,700
once it takes the
wrong action and it

1632
01:17:23,700 --> 01:17:26,550
doesn't have the search
ability to understand,

1633
01:17:26,550 --> 01:17:27,420
I'm not actually--

1634
01:17:27,420 --> 01:17:31,300
I've taken the action that is
not consistent with my task,

1635
01:17:31,300 --> 01:17:33,730
then it's very hard for it
to backtrack potentially.

1636
01:17:33,730 --> 01:17:35,820
Once you're taking the action,
it's trying to take another

1637
01:17:35,820 --> 01:17:37,362
action, maybe it's
trying to recover,

1638
01:17:37,362 --> 01:17:41,520
and then basically cannot
recover back to the original

1639
01:17:41,520 --> 01:17:43,070
set.

